window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2018-06-2", "source": "2018年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of building trust between teachers and students. You can cite examples to illustrate your views. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Trust is the invisible bridge that connects a teacher and a student. In my view, building mutual trust between\nthe two plays a vital role in effective education, and its importance can hardly be overstated.\nTo begin with, trust encourages students to open up. When learners believe their teacher will not laugh at them,\nthey dare to ask questions and admit confusion, which greatly improves learning efficiency. Take my former\nclassmate Tom as an example. He used to keep silent in class, yet after a patient teacher earned his trust, he\nbegan to raise his hand and made remarkable progress. Moreover, trust fosters a positive learning atmosphere,\nin which teachers are willing to devote extra effort and students feel respected rather than judged.\nIn conclusion, mutual trust is not a luxury but a necessity for genuine learning. Only when teachers and\nstudents truly rely on each other can knowledge be passed on and talent be nurtured. Therefore, both sides\nshould make every effort to establish and protect this precious bond."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中国目前拥有世界上最大最快的高速铁路网。高铁列车的运行速度还将继续提升，更多的城市将修建高铁站。高铁大大缩短了人们出行的时间。相对飞机而言，高铁列车的突出优势在于准时，因为基本不受天气或交通管制的影响。高铁极大地改变了中国人的生活方式。如今，它已经成了很多人商务旅行的首选交通工具。越来越多的人也在假日乘高铁外出旅游。还有不少年轻人选择在一个城市工作而在邻近城市居住，每天乘高铁上下班。", "sample": "China currently has the largest and fastest high-speed railway network in the world. The speed of high-speed trains will continue to increase, and more cities will build high-speed rail stations. High-speed rail has greatly reduced people's travel time. Compared with airplanes, the outstanding advantage of high- speed trains is punctuality, because they are rarely affected by weather or traffic control. High-speed rail has greatly changed the lifestyle of Chinese people. Today, it has become the first choice of transportation for business travel for many people. More and more people also travel by high-speed train during holidays. There are also many young people who choose to work in one city and live in a neighboring one, commuting between the two cities by high-speed train every day."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2018-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Did Sarah Josepha Hale write \"Mary's Little Lamb,\" the eternal nursery rhyme(儿歌) about a girl named Mary with a stubborn lamb? This is still disputed, but it's clear that the woman  __26__  for writing it was one of America's most fascinating  __27__  . In honor of the poem's publication on May 24, 1830, here's more about the  __28__  author's life.\nHale wasn't just a writer, she was also a  __29__  social advocate, and she was particularly  __30__  with an ideal New England, which she associated with abundant Thanksgiving meals that she claimed had \"a deep moral influence.\" She began a nationwide  __31__  to have a national holiday declared that would bring families together while celebrating the  __32__  festivals. In 1863, after 17 years of advocacy including letters to five presidents, Hale got it. President Abraham Lincoln, during the Civil War, issued a  __33__  setting aside the last Thursday in November for the holiday.\nThe true authorship of \"Mary's Little Lamb\" is disputed. According to the New England Historical Society, Hale wrote only part of the poem, but claimed authorship. Regardless of the author, it seems that the poem was  __34__  by a real event. When young Mary Sawyer was followed to school by a lamb in 1816, it caused some problems. A bystander named John Roulstone wrote a poem about the event, then, at some point, Hale herself seems to have helped write it. However, if a 1916 piece by her great-niece is to be trusted, Hale claimed for the  __35__  of her life that \"some other people pretended that someone else wrote the poem\".", "matchIntro": "Grow Plants Without Water", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Ever since humanity began to farm our own food, we've faced the unpredictable rain that is both friend and enemy. It comes and goes without much warning, and a field of lush(茂盛的) leafy greens one year can dry up and blow away the next. Food security and fortunes depend on sufficient rain, and nowhere more so than in Africa, where 96% of farmland depends on rain instead of the irrigation common in more developed places. It has consequences: South Africa's ongoing drought—the worst in three decades—will cost at least a quarter of its corn crop this year."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Biologist Jill Farrant of the University of Cape Town in South Africa says that nature has plenty of answers for people who want to grow crops in places with unpredictable rainfall. She is hard at work finding a way to take traits from rare wild plants that adapt to extreme dry weather and use them in food crops. As the earth's climate changes and rainfall becomes even less predictable in some places, those answers will grow even more valuable. \"The type of farming I'm aiming for is literally so that people can survive as it's going to get more and more dry,\" Farrant says."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Extreme conditions produce extremely tough plants. In the rusty red deserts of South Africa, steep-sided rocky hills called inselbergs rear up from the plains like the bones of the earth. The hills are remnants of an earlier geological era, scraped bare of most soil and exposed to the elements. Yet on these and similar formations in deserts around the world, a few fierce plants have adapted to endure under ever-changing conditions."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Farrant calls them resurrection plants(复苏植物). During months without water under a harsh sun, they wither, shrink and contract until they look like a pile of dead gray leaves. But rainfall can revive them in a matter of hours. Her time-lapse(间歇性拍摄的) videos of the revivals look like someone playing a tape of the plant's death in reverse."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The big difference between \"drought-tolerant\" plants and these tough plants: metabolism. Many different kinds of plants have developed tactics to weather dry spells. Some plants store reserves of water to see them through a drought; others send roots deep down to subsurface water supplies. But once these plants use up their stored reserve or tap out the underground supply, they cease growing and start to die. They may be able to handle a drought of some length, and many people use the term \"drought tolerant\" to describe such plants, but they never actually stop needing to consume water, so Farrant prefers to call them drought resistant."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Resurrection plants, defined as those capable of recovering from holding less than 0.1 grams of water per gram of dry mass, are different. They lack water-storing structures, and their existence on rock faces prevents them from tapping groundwater, so they have instead developed the ability to change their metabolism. When they detect an extended dry period, they divert their metabolisms, producing sugars and certain stress-associated proteins and other materials in their tissues. As the plant dries, these resources take on first the properties of honey, then rubber, and finally enter a glass-like state that is \"the most stable state that the plant can maintain,\" Farrant says. That slows the plant's metabolism and protects its dried-out tissues. The plants also change shape, shrinking to minimize the surface area through which their remaining water might evaporate. They can recover from months and years without water, depending on the species."}, {"label": "G", "text": "What else can do this dry out and revive trick? Seeds—almost all of them. At the start of her career, Farrant studied \"recalcitrant seeds(顽拗性种子),\" such as avocados, coffee and lychee. While tasty, such seeds are delicate—they cannot bud and grow if they dry out (as you may know if you've ever tried to grow a tree from an avocado pit). In the seed world, that makes them rare, because most seeds from flowering plants are quite robust. Most seeds can wait out the dry, unwelcoming seasons until conditions are right and they sprout(发芽). Yet once they start growing, such plants seem not to retain the ability to hit the pause button on metabolism in their stems or leaves."}, {"label": "H", "text": "After completing her Ph. D. on seeds, Farrant began investigating whether it might be possible to isolate the properties that make most seeds so resilient(迅速恢复活力的) and transfer them to other plant tissues. What Farrant and others have found over the past two decades is that there are many genes involved in resurrection plants' response to dryness. Many of them are the same that regulate how seeds become dryness-tolerant while still attached to their parent plants. Now they are trying to figure out what molecular signaling processes activate those seed-building genes in resurrection plants—and how to reproduce them in crops. \"Most genes are regulated by a master set of genes,\" Farrant says. \"We're looking at gene promoters and what would be their master switch.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Once Farrant and her colleagues feel they have a better sense of which switches to throw, they will have to find the best way to do so in useful crops. \"I'm trying three methods of breeding,\" Farrant says: conventional, genetic modification and gene editing. She says she is aware that plenty of people do not want to eat genetically modified crops, but she is pushing ahead with every available tool until one works. Farmers and consumers alike can choose whether or not to use whichever version prevails: \"I'm giving people an option.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Farrant and others in the resurrection business got together last year to discuss the best species of resurrection plant to use as a lab model. Just like medical researchers use rats to test ideas for human medical treatments, botanists use plants that are relatively easy to grow in a lab or greenhouse setting to test their ideas for related species. The Queensland rock violet is one of the best studied resurrection plants so far, with a draft genome(基因图谱) published last year by a Chinese team. Also last year, Farrant and colleagues published a detailed molecular study of another candidate, Xerophyta viscosa, a tough-as-nail South African plant with lily-like flowers, and she says that a genome is on the way. One or both of these models will help researchers test their ideas—so far mostly done in the lab—on test plots."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Understanding the basic science first is key. There are good reasons why crop plants do not use dryness defenses already. For instance, there's a high energy cost in switching from a regular metabolism to an almost-no-water metabolism. It will also be necessary to understand what sort of yield farmers might expect and to establish the plant's safety. \"The yield is never going to be high,\" Farrant says, so these plants will be targeted not at Iowa farmers trying to squeeze more cash out of high-yield fields, but subsistence farmers who need help to survive a drought like the present one in South Africa. \"My vision is for the subsistence farmer,\" Farrant says. \"I'm targeting crops that are of African value.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Human memory is notoriously unreliable. Even people with the sharpest facial-recognition skills can only remember so much.\nIt's tough to quantify how good a person is at remembering. No one really knows how many different faces someone can recall, for example, but various estimates tend to hover in the thousands—based on the number of acquaintances a person might have.\nMachines aren't limited this way. Give the right computer a massive database of faces, and it can process what it sees—then recognize a face it's told to find—with remarkable speed and precision. This skill is what supports the enormous promise of facial-recognition software in the 21st century. It's also what makes contemporary surveillance systems so scary.\nThe thing is, machines still have limitations when it comes to facial recognition. And scientists are only just beginning to understand what those constraints are. To begin to figure out how computers are struggling, researchers at the University of Washington created a massive database of faces—they call it MegaFace—and tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in complexity. The idea was to test the machines on a database that included up to 1 million different images of nearly 700,000 different people—and not just a large database featuring a relatively small number of different faces, more consistent with what's been used in other research.\nAs the databases grew, machine accuracy dipped across the board. Algorithms that were right 95% of the time when they were dealing with a 13,000-image database, for example, were accurate about 70% of the time when confronted with 1 million images. That's still pretty good, says one of the researchers, Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman. \"Much better than we expected,\" she said.\nMachines also had difficulty adjusting for people who look a lot alike—either doppelgangers(长相极相似的人), whom the machine would have trouble identifying as two separate people, or the same person who appeared in different photos at different ages or in different lighting, whom the machine would incorrectly view as separate people.\n\"Once we scale up, algorithms must be sensitive to tiny changes in identities and at the same time invariant to lighting, pose, age,\" Kemelmacher-Shlizerman said.\nThe trouble is, for many of the researchers who'd like to design systems to address these challenges, massive datasets for experimentation just don't exist—at least, not in formats that are accessible to academic researchers. Training sets like the ones Google and Facebook have are private. There are no public databases that contain millions of faces. Mega Face's creators say it's the largest publicly available facial-recognition dataset out there.\n\"An ultimate face recognition algorithm should perform with billions of people in a dataset,\" the researchers wrote.", "47": "Human memory is notoriously unreliable. Even people with the sharpest facial-recognition skills can only remember so much.\nIt's tough to quantify how good a person is at remembering. No one really knows how many different faces someone can recall, for example, but various estimates tend to hover in the thousands—based on the number of acquaintances a person might have.\nMachines aren't limited this way. Give the right computer a massive database of faces, and it can process what it sees—then recognize a face it's told to find—with remarkable speed and precision. This skill is what supports the enormous promise of facial-recognition software in the 21st century. It's also what makes contemporary surveillance systems so scary.\nThe thing is, machines still have limitations when it comes to facial recognition. And scientists are only just beginning to understand what those constraints are. To begin to figure out how computers are struggling, researchers at the University of Washington created a massive database of faces—they call it MegaFace—and tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in complexity. The idea was to test the machines on a database that included up to 1 million different images of nearly 700,000 different people—and not just a large database featuring a relatively small number of different faces, more consistent with what's been used in other research.\nAs the databases grew, machine accuracy dipped across the board. Algorithms that were right 95% of the time when they were dealing with a 13,000-image database, for example, were accurate about 70% of the time when confronted with 1 million images. That's still pretty good, says one of the researchers, Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman. \"Much better than we expected,\" she said.\nMachines also had difficulty adjusting for people who look a lot alike—either doppelgangers(长相极相似的人), whom the machine would have trouble identifying as two separate people, or the same person who appeared in different photos at different ages or in different lighting, whom the machine would incorrectly view as separate people.\n\"Once we scale up, algorithms must be sensitive to tiny changes in identities and at the same time invariant to lighting, pose, age,\" Kemelmacher-Shlizerman said.\nThe trouble is, for many of the researchers who'd like to design systems to address these challenges, massive datasets for experimentation just don't exist—at least, not in formats that are accessible to academic researchers. Training sets like the ones Google and Facebook have are private. There are no public databases that contain millions of faces. Mega Face's creators say it's the largest publicly available facial-recognition dataset out there.\n\"An ultimate face recognition algorithm should perform with billions of people in a dataset,\" the researchers wrote.", "48": "Human memory is notoriously unreliable. Even people with the sharpest facial-recognition skills can only remember so much.\nIt's tough to quantify how good a person is at remembering. No one really knows how many different faces someone can recall, for example, but various estimates tend to hover in the thousands—based on the number of acquaintances a person might have.\nMachines aren't limited this way. Give the right computer a massive database of faces, and it can process what it sees—then recognize a face it's told to find—with remarkable speed and precision. This skill is what supports the enormous promise of facial-recognition software in the 21st century. It's also what makes contemporary surveillance systems so scary.\nThe thing is, machines still have limitations when it comes to facial recognition. And scientists are only just beginning to understand what those constraints are. To begin to figure out how computers are struggling, researchers at the University of Washington created a massive database of faces—they call it MegaFace—and tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in complexity. The idea was to test the machines on a database that included up to 1 million different images of nearly 700,000 different people—and not just a large database featuring a relatively small number of different faces, more consistent with what's been used in other research.\nAs the databases grew, machine accuracy dipped across the board. Algorithms that were right 95% of the time when they were dealing with a 13,000-image database, for example, were accurate about 70% of the time when confronted with 1 million images. That's still pretty good, says one of the researchers, Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman. \"Much better than we expected,\" she said.\nMachines also had difficulty adjusting for people who look a lot alike—either doppelgangers(长相极相似的人), whom the machine would have trouble identifying as two separate people, or the same person who appeared in different photos at different ages or in different lighting, whom the machine would incorrectly view as separate people.\n\"Once we scale up, algorithms must be sensitive to tiny changes in identities and at the same time invariant to lighting, pose, age,\" Kemelmacher-Shlizerman said.\nThe trouble is, for many of the researchers who'd like to design systems to address these challenges, massive datasets for experimentation just don't exist—at least, not in formats that are accessible to academic researchers. Training sets like the ones Google and Facebook have are private. There are no public databases that contain millions of faces. Mega Face's creators say it's the largest publicly available facial-recognition dataset out there.\n\"An ultimate face recognition algorithm should perform with billions of people in a dataset,\" the researchers wrote.", "49": "Human memory is notoriously unreliable. Even people with the sharpest facial-recognition skills can only remember so much.\nIt's tough to quantify how good a person is at remembering. No one really knows how many different faces someone can recall, for example, but various estimates tend to hover in the thousands—based on the number of acquaintances a person might have.\nMachines aren't limited this way. Give the right computer a massive database of faces, and it can process what it sees—then recognize a face it's told to find—with remarkable speed and precision. This skill is what supports the enormous promise of facial-recognition software in the 21st century. It's also what makes contemporary surveillance systems so scary.\nThe thing is, machines still have limitations when it comes to facial recognition. And scientists are only just beginning to understand what those constraints are. To begin to figure out how computers are struggling, researchers at the University of Washington created a massive database of faces—they call it MegaFace—and tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in complexity. The idea was to test the machines on a database that included up to 1 million different images of nearly 700,000 different people—and not just a large database featuring a relatively small number of different faces, more consistent with what's been used in other research.\nAs the databases grew, machine accuracy dipped across the board. Algorithms that were right 95% of the time when they were dealing with a 13,000-image database, for example, were accurate about 70% of the time when confronted with 1 million images. That's still pretty good, says one of the researchers, Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman. \"Much better than we expected,\" she said.\nMachines also had difficulty adjusting for people who look a lot alike—either doppelgangers(长相极相似的人), whom the machine would have trouble identifying as two separate people, or the same person who appeared in different photos at different ages or in different lighting, whom the machine would incorrectly view as separate people.\n\"Once we scale up, algorithms must be sensitive to tiny changes in identities and at the same time invariant to lighting, pose, age,\" Kemelmacher-Shlizerman said.\nThe trouble is, for many of the researchers who'd like to design systems to address these challenges, massive datasets for experimentation just don't exist—at least, not in formats that are accessible to academic researchers. Training sets like the ones Google and Facebook have are private. There are no public databases that contain millions of faces. Mega Face's creators say it's the largest publicly available facial-recognition dataset out there.\n\"An ultimate face recognition algorithm should perform with billions of people in a dataset,\" the researchers wrote.", "50": "Human memory is notoriously unreliable. Even people with the sharpest facial-recognition skills can only remember so much.\nIt's tough to quantify how good a person is at remembering. No one really knows how many different faces someone can recall, for example, but various estimates tend to hover in the thousands—based on the number of acquaintances a person might have.\nMachines aren't limited this way. Give the right computer a massive database of faces, and it can process what it sees—then recognize a face it's told to find—with remarkable speed and precision. This skill is what supports the enormous promise of facial-recognition software in the 21st century. It's also what makes contemporary surveillance systems so scary.\nThe thing is, machines still have limitations when it comes to facial recognition. And scientists are only just beginning to understand what those constraints are. To begin to figure out how computers are struggling, researchers at the University of Washington created a massive database of faces—they call it MegaFace—and tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in complexity. The idea was to test the machines on a database that included up to 1 million different images of nearly 700,000 different people—and not just a large database featuring a relatively small number of different faces, more consistent with what's been used in other research.\nAs the databases grew, machine accuracy dipped across the board. Algorithms that were right 95% of the time when they were dealing with a 13,000-image database, for example, were accurate about 70% of the time when confronted with 1 million images. That's still pretty good, says one of the researchers, Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman. \"Much better than we expected,\" she said.\nMachines also had difficulty adjusting for people who look a lot alike—either doppelgangers(长相极相似的人), whom the machine would have trouble identifying as two separate people, or the same person who appeared in different photos at different ages or in different lighting, whom the machine would incorrectly view as separate people.\n\"Once we scale up, algorithms must be sensitive to tiny changes in identities and at the same time invariant to lighting, pose, age,\" Kemelmacher-Shlizerman said.\nThe trouble is, for many of the researchers who'd like to design systems to address these challenges, massive datasets for experimentation just don't exist—at least, not in formats that are accessible to academic researchers. Training sets like the ones Google and Facebook have are private. There are no public databases that contain millions of faces. Mega Face's creators say it's the largest publicly available facial-recognition dataset out there.\n\"An ultimate face recognition algorithm should perform with billions of people in a dataset,\" the researchers wrote.", "51": "There are currently 21.5 million students in America, and many will be funding their college on borrowed money. Given that there's now over $1.3 trillion in student loans on the books, it's pretty clear that many students are far from sensible. The average student's debt upon graduation now approaches $40,000, and as college becomes ever more expensive, calls to make it \"free\" are multiplying. Even Hillary Clinton says that when it comes to college, \"Costs won't be a barrier.\"\nBut the only way college could be free is if the faculty and staff donated their time, the buildings required no maintenance, and campuses required no utilities. As long as it's impossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier.\nThe actual question we debate is who should pay for people to go to college. If taxpayers are to bear the cost of forgiving student loans, shouldn't they have a say in how their money is used?\nAt least taxpayers should be able to decide what students will study on the public dime. If we're going to force taxpayers to foot the bill for college degrees, students should only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers. After all, students making their own choices in this respect is what caused the problem in the first place. We simply don't need more poetry, gender studies, or sociology majors. How do we know which subjects benefit society? Easy.\nAverage starting salaries give a clear indication of what type of training society needs its new workers to have. Certainly, there are benefits to a college major beyond the job a student can perform. But if we're talking about the benefits to society, the only thing that matters is what the major enables the student to produce for society. And the value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing to pay the student to produce it.\nA low wage for elementary school teachers, however, doesn't mean elementary education isn't important. It simply means there are too many elementary school teachers already.\nMeanwhile, there are few who are willing and able to perform jobs requiring a petroleum engineering major, so the value of one more of those people is very high.\nSo we can have taxpayers pick up students' tuition in exchange for dictating what those students will study. Or we can allow students both to choose their majors and pay for their education themselves. But in the end, one of two things is true:\nEither a college major is worth its cost or it isn't. If yes, taxpayer financing isn't needed. If not, taxpayer financing isn't desirable. Either way, taxpayers have no business paying for students' college education.", "52": "There are currently 21.5 million students in America, and many will be funding their college on borrowed money. Given that there's now over $1.3 trillion in student loans on the books, it's pretty clear that many students are far from sensible. The average student's debt upon graduation now approaches $40,000, and as college becomes ever more expensive, calls to make it \"free\" are multiplying. Even Hillary Clinton says that when it comes to college, \"Costs won't be a barrier.\"\nBut the only way college could be free is if the faculty and staff donated their time, the buildings required no maintenance, and campuses required no utilities. As long as it's impossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier.\nThe actual question we debate is who should pay for people to go to college. If taxpayers are to bear the cost of forgiving student loans, shouldn't they have a say in how their money is used?\nAt least taxpayers should be able to decide what students will study on the public dime. If we're going to force taxpayers to foot the bill for college degrees, students should only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers. After all, students making their own choices in this respect is what caused the problem in the first place. We simply don't need more poetry, gender studies, or sociology majors. How do we know which subjects benefit society? Easy.\nAverage starting salaries give a clear indication of what type of training society needs its new workers to have. Certainly, there are benefits to a college major beyond the job a student can perform. But if we're talking about the benefits to society, the only thing that matters is what the major enables the student to produce for society. And the value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing to pay the student to produce it.\nA low wage for elementary school teachers, however, doesn't mean elementary education isn't important. It simply means there are too many elementary school teachers already.\nMeanwhile, there are few who are willing and able to perform jobs requiring a petroleum engineering major, so the value of one more of those people is very high.\nSo we can have taxpayers pick up students' tuition in exchange for dictating what those students will study. Or we can allow students both to choose their majors and pay for their education themselves. But in the end, one of two things is true:\nEither a college major is worth its cost or it isn't. If yes, taxpayer financing isn't needed. If not, taxpayer financing isn't desirable. Either way, taxpayers have no business paying for students' college education.", "53": "There are currently 21.5 million students in America, and many will be funding their college on borrowed money. Given that there's now over $1.3 trillion in student loans on the books, it's pretty clear that many students are far from sensible. The average student's debt upon graduation now approaches $40,000, and as college becomes ever more expensive, calls to make it \"free\" are multiplying. Even Hillary Clinton says that when it comes to college, \"Costs won't be a barrier.\"\nBut the only way college could be free is if the faculty and staff donated their time, the buildings required no maintenance, and campuses required no utilities. As long as it's impossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier.\nThe actual question we debate is who should pay for people to go to college. If taxpayers are to bear the cost of forgiving student loans, shouldn't they have a say in how their money is used?\nAt least taxpayers should be able to decide what students will study on the public dime. If we're going to force taxpayers to foot the bill for college degrees, students should only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers. After all, students making their own choices in this respect is what caused the problem in the first place. We simply don't need more poetry, gender studies, or sociology majors. How do we know which subjects benefit society? Easy.\nAverage starting salaries give a clear indication of what type of training society needs its new workers to have. Certainly, there are benefits to a college major beyond the job a student can perform. But if we're talking about the benefits to society, the only thing that matters is what the major enables the student to produce for society. And the value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing to pay the student to produce it.\nA low wage for elementary school teachers, however, doesn't mean elementary education isn't important. It simply means there are too many elementary school teachers already.\nMeanwhile, there are few who are willing and able to perform jobs requiring a petroleum engineering major, so the value of one more of those people is very high.\nSo we can have taxpayers pick up students' tuition in exchange for dictating what those students will study. Or we can allow students both to choose their majors and pay for their education themselves. But in the end, one of two things is true:\nEither a college major is worth its cost or it isn't. If yes, taxpayer financing isn't needed. If not, taxpayer financing isn't desirable. Either way, taxpayers have no business paying for students' college education.", "54": "There are currently 21.5 million students in America, and many will be funding their college on borrowed money. Given that there's now over $1.3 trillion in student loans on the books, it's pretty clear that many students are far from sensible. The average student's debt upon graduation now approaches $40,000, and as college becomes ever more expensive, calls to make it \"free\" are multiplying. Even Hillary Clinton says that when it comes to college, \"Costs won't be a barrier.\"\nBut the only way college could be free is if the faculty and staff donated their time, the buildings required no maintenance, and campuses required no utilities. As long as it's impossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier.\nThe actual question we debate is who should pay for people to go to college. If taxpayers are to bear the cost of forgiving student loans, shouldn't they have a say in how their money is used?\nAt least taxpayers should be able to decide what students will study on the public dime. If we're going to force taxpayers to foot the bill for college degrees, students should only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers. After all, students making their own choices in this respect is what caused the problem in the first place. We simply don't need more poetry, gender studies, or sociology majors. How do we know which subjects benefit society? Easy.\nAverage starting salaries give a clear indication of what type of training society needs its new workers to have. Certainly, there are benefits to a college major beyond the job a student can perform. But if we're talking about the benefits to society, the only thing that matters is what the major enables the student to produce for society. And the value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing to pay the student to produce it.\nA low wage for elementary school teachers, however, doesn't mean elementary education isn't important. It simply means there are too many elementary school teachers already.\nMeanwhile, there are few who are willing and able to perform jobs requiring a petroleum engineering major, so the value of one more of those people is very high.\nSo we can have taxpayers pick up students' tuition in exchange for dictating what those students will study. Or we can allow students both to choose their majors and pay for their education themselves. But in the end, one of two things is true:\nEither a college major is worth its cost or it isn't. If yes, taxpayer financing isn't needed. If not, taxpayer financing isn't desirable. Either way, taxpayers have no business paying for students' college education.", "55": "There are currently 21.5 million students in America, and many will be funding their college on borrowed money. Given that there's now over $1.3 trillion in student loans on the books, it's pretty clear that many students are far from sensible. The average student's debt upon graduation now approaches $40,000, and as college becomes ever more expensive, calls to make it \"free\" are multiplying. Even Hillary Clinton says that when it comes to college, \"Costs won't be a barrier.\"\nBut the only way college could be free is if the faculty and staff donated their time, the buildings required no maintenance, and campuses required no utilities. As long as it's impossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier.\nThe actual question we debate is who should pay for people to go to college. If taxpayers are to bear the cost of forgiving student loans, shouldn't they have a say in how their money is used?\nAt least taxpayers should be able to decide what students will study on the public dime. If we're going to force taxpayers to foot the bill for college degrees, students should only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers. After all, students making their own choices in this respect is what caused the problem in the first place. We simply don't need more poetry, gender studies, or sociology majors. How do we know which subjects benefit society? Easy.\nAverage starting salaries give a clear indication of what type of training society needs its new workers to have. Certainly, there are benefits to a college major beyond the job a student can perform. But if we're talking about the benefits to society, the only thing that matters is what the major enables the student to produce for society. And the value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing to pay the student to produce it.\nA low wage for elementary school teachers, however, doesn't mean elementary education isn't important. It simply means there are too many elementary school teachers already.\nMeanwhile, there are few who are willing and able to perform jobs requiring a petroleum engineering major, so the value of one more of those people is very high.\nSo we can have taxpayers pick up students' tuition in exchange for dictating what those students will study. Or we can allow students both to choose their majors and pay for their education themselves. But in the end, one of two things is true:\nEither a college major is worth its cost or it isn't. If yes, taxpayer financing isn't needed. If not, taxpayer financing isn't desirable. Either way, taxpayers have no business paying for students' college education."}, "questions": [{"id": "2018-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman?", "options": ["A) She advocates animal protection.", "B) She sells a special kind of coffee.", "C) She is going to start a café chain.", "D) She is the owner of a special café."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She is the owner of a special café.\n【定位】Tonight we have a special guest from a local establishment, the Parisian Cafe.\n今晚我们请到了一位来自本地一家店——巴黎咖啡馆——的特别嘉宾。\n【信号】开场人物引入句。主持人 Tonight we have a special guest from a local establishment,\nthe Parisian Cafe. 这句把嘉宾与“巴黎咖啡馆”绑定，是典型的开场身份介绍信号；再结合后\n文她一直用 we have rabbits wandering freely around the place. 的 we 自指这家店，可\n确认她就是店主。\n【替换】a special guest from a local establishment, the Parisian Cafe ↔ the owner of a special café\n“来自这家店的嘉宾+全程 we”=店主\n【排除】A) 未提及\n她确实养动物、说顾客都是爱动物的人，但全程没说自己倡导 animal protection；She\nadvocates animal protection. 是借“店里有动物”过度联想出来的身份，原文无据。\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文有 offer something a little special 和咖啡馆场景，She sells a special kind of\ncoffee. 就把 special 和 coffee 这两个真被念到的词拼起来，但她卖的特色是“店里有兔\n子”，不是“一种特别的咖啡”，答非所问。\n借用原文：offer something a little special and different from other establishments\nC) 未提及\n原文只谈她现有这一家特色店，从未提扩张或连锁；She is going to start a café chain. 的\ncafé chain 与将来计划都是无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the woman say about cafes in her city?", "options": ["A) They bear a lot of similarities.", "B) They are a profitable business sector.", "C) They cater to different customers.", "D) They help take care of customers' pets."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They bear a lot of similarities.\n【定位】There are more than plenty in our city, and we felt they are all rather similar to each other.\n我们城里咖啡馆多得很，而我们觉得它们彼此之间都相当相似。\n【信号】她对同行下的直接判断句。we felt they are all rather similar to each other 是受访者亲口\n给本市咖啡馆下的定性，用了 we felt 引出观点、they 指代这些店，正是被问者应答里承载\n答案的那句。\n【替换】they are all rather similar to each other ↔ They bear a lot of similarities.\n形容词 similar 名词化为 similarities\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 Cafes certainly are a very competitive market sector.，They are a profitable\nbusiness sector. 就借 sector 一词制造熟悉感，但原文说的是 competitive（竞争激烈），\n不是 profitable（盈利），偷换了关键定性。\n借用原文：Cafes certainly are a very competitive market sector.\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明说这些店 all rather similar to each other，They cater to different customers. 却\n说它们面向“不同”顾客，恰好与“相似”相反，是预读时就标出的相反陷阱。\n借用原文：they are all rather similar to each other\nD) 未提及\n原文谈的是店里养兔子供顾客体验，从没说这些咖啡馆帮顾客照看宠物；They help take\ncare of customers' pets. 是把“店里有动物”错接成“代管宠物”，无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "How does the Parisian cafe guarantee that its rabbits pose no health threat?", "options": ["A) By giving them regular cleaning and injections.", "B) By selecting breeds that are tame and peaceful.", "C) By placing them at a safe distance from customers.", "D) By briefing customers on how to get along with them."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） By giving them regular cleaning and injections.\n【定位】Furthermore, our rabbits are regularly cleaned and have all received the required shots. So\nthere is no health risk whatsoever.\n而且，我们的兔子都定期清洁，并且都打了规定的疫苗。所以根本没有任何健康风险。\n【信号】Furthermore 引出的措施 + health risk 收束。Furthermore, our rabbits are regularly\ncleaned and have all received the required shots. 由 Furthermore 递进引出具体做法，\n紧接 So there is no health risk whatsoever. 用 health risk 点回题干的 health threat，一\n前一后正好框定这就是“保证健康无虞”的答案句。\n【替换】our rabbits are regularly cleaned ↔ regular cleaning\n被动句变名词短语\nhave all received the required shots ↔ injections\nshots = injections 同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文确说 Rabbits are very peaceful and certainly do not bite.，By selecting breeds\nthat are tame and peaceful. 就借 peaceful 一词诱选，但那是在说兔子不咬人（安全），\n且并非“挑选温顺品种”这一办法，与题干问的“健康”更不对路。\n借用原文：Rabbits are very peaceful and certainly do not bite.\nC) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说兔子 play about and brush against their legs，是自由地蹭到顾客腿边、与人\n亲近；By placing them at a safe distance from customers. 说让兔子与顾客保持安全距\n离，与原文场景正好相反。\n借用原文：little rabbits play about and brush against their legs\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文只说咖啡馆给孩子 a chance for them to learn about rabbits and how to take care\nof them，是面向孩子的体验，并非通过“告知顾客如何与兔子相处”来保证健康；By\nbriefing customers on how to get along with them. 把对象与目的都接错了。\n借用原文：a chance for them to learn about rabbits and how to take care of them", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about their customers?", "options": ["A) They want to learn about rabbits.", "B) They like to bring in their children.", "C) They love the animals in her café.", "D) They give her café favorite reviews."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They love the animals in her café.\n【定位】As for our customers, they are all animal lovers, so they would never try to hurt the rabbits.\n至于我们的顾客，他们全都是爱动物的人，所以绝不会去伤害兔子。\n【信号】As for our customers 引出的总评句。As for our customers, they are all animal lovers\n用 As for 把话题切到“顾客”，紧跟的 they are all animal lovers 就是她对这群人下的整体\n定性，是承载本题答案的那句。\n【替换】they are all animal lovers ↔ They love the animals in her café.\n名词 animal lovers = 动词 love the animals\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文 a chance for them to learn about rabbits 说的是“给孩子一个了解兔子的机会”，主\n体是 children；They want to learn about rabbits. 把这句安到 customers（顾客）头\n上，对象错位，且原文是“提供机会”而非顾客“想学”。\n借用原文：a chance for them to learn about rabbits and how to take care of them\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文提到 Sometimes a young child may get over-excited，是举例说孩子有时太兴奋，\n并未说顾客“喜欢带孩子来”；They like to bring in their children. 是把 child 这个词重新包\n装成一条原文没有的顾客习惯。\n借用原文：Sometimes a young child may get over-excited\nD) 未提及\n全文没有任何顾客评价/打分的内容；They give her café favorite reviews. 的 favorite\nreviews 是无中生有，靠“顾客满意”的氛围诱人脑补。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do the speakers say about the food their children like?", "options": ["A) It contains too many additives.", "B) It lacks the essential vitamins.", "C) It can cause obesity.", "D) It is mostly garbage."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is mostly garbage.\n【定位】It's never a carrot or a peach. It's always some garbage, like chocolate-covered sweets.\n永远不会是胡萝卜或桃子，永远是些垃圾，比如裹着巧克力的糖果。\n【信号】贬义定性词 garbage。男士用 It's always some garbage 直接给孩子爱吃的食物下了\"垃\n圾\"这一定性。这种\"对某物的直接定性词\"就是细节评价题的答案信号，听到 garbage 这种\n带强烈贬义的概括词就锁定。\n【替换】It's always some garbage ↔ It is mostly garbage.\nalways 与 mostly 同义弱化\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇未提 additives（添加剂），只笼统说 garbage、unhealthy。这是凭\"垃圾食品听\n起来就有添加剂\"的常识脑补，原文并无依据。\nB) 过度推断\n录音只说食物 unhealthy、是 garbage，从未说它 lacks the essential vitamins（缺乏必\n需维生素）。这是把\"不健康\"过度具体化成\"缺维生素\"。\nC) 过度推断\n录音没有把孩子爱吃的食物与 obesity（肥胖）这一后果挂钩，全程只停\n在\"unhealthy/garbage\"层面。把\"垃圾食品\"自然联想到\"导致肥胖\"是常识，不是原文所\n说。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "According to the speakers, what affects children's choice of food most?", "options": ["A) Its fancy design.", "B) TV commercials.", "C) Its taste and texture.", "D) Peer influence."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） TV commercials.\n【定位】I think it's all that advertising on TV. That's where they get it.\n我觉得就是电视上那些广告闹的。他们就是从那儿学来的。\n【信号】强调结构 it's all that。女士用 I think it's all that advertising on TV 的强调句式把成因一\n口咬定在电视广告上，紧接 That's where they get it 再次确认。这种\"it's all that…/That's\nwhere…\"的归因强调就是\"最主要原因\"的答案信号。\n【替换】advertising on TV ↔ TV commercials.\nadvertising=commercials 同义词\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从未提到食物的 fancy design（精美设计/包装）影响孩子，归因只落在广告上。这是\n凭\"包装好看吸引小孩\"的常识脑补出来的因素。\nC) 未提及\n录音没有把 taste and texture（口味与口感）列为影响选择的因素，更没说它\"最\"主要。这\n是把\"小孩爱吃当然因为好吃\"的常识当成了原文。\nD) 未提及\n录音通篇没提 peer influence（同伴影响）。它利用了\"小孩互相攀比\"的生活经验设套，但\n原文归因始终是广告，不是同伴。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do the speakers believe big food corporations are doing?", "options": ["A) Investing heavily in the production of sweet foods.", "B) Marketing their products with ordinary ingredients.", "C) Trying to trick children into buying their products.", "D) Offering children more varieties to choose from."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Trying to trick children into buying their products.\n【定位】And these big food corporations have so much money to spend on clever tactics designed to\nmake young children want to buy their products. Children never stand a chance.\n而且这些大型食品公司有大把的钱花在精心设计的手段上，就为了让小孩子想买他们的产\n品。孩子们根本没有招架之力。\n【信号】贬义定性 clever tactics。女士说商家把钱花在 clever tactics designed to make young\nchildren want to buy their products，再补 Children never stand a chance，强烈暗示\n这是\"算计孩子\"。这种\"对行为的贬义定性 + 加重语气\"就是态度类细节题的答案信号。\n【替换】clever tactics designed to make young children want to buy their products ↔\nTrying to trick children into buying their products.\ntactics 概括为 trick\nChildren never stand a chance ↔ trick children\n补刀句坐实欺骗\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说商家 have so much money to spend，但花在 tactics（手段）上，不是\nInvesting heavily in the production of sweet foods（重金投入甜食生产）。它盗用\"很多\n钱\"这个原词，却把去向偷换成\"生产\"。\n借用原文：have so much money to spend on clever tactics\nB) 与原文相反\nMarketing their products with ordinary ingredients（用普通食材营销）与原文相反：上\n文 One never sees ordinary ingredients being advertised 明说普通食材根本不被广告，\n商家卖的恰是 garbage。它盗 ordinary ingredients 一词却把意思反了。\n借用原文：One never sees ordinary ingredients being advertised on TV\nD) 与原文相反\nOffering children more varieties to choose from（给孩子更多选择）把商家说成在做好\n事，与录音\"设套算计孩子\"的负面定性完全相反，录音也根本没提\"更多选择\"。\n借用原文：clever tactics designed to make young children want to buy their products", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do we know about the speakers when they were children?", "options": ["A) They hardly ate vegetables.", "B) They seldom had junk food.", "C) They favored chocolate-coated sweets.", "D) They liked the food advertised on TV."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They seldom had junk food.\n【定位】When we were children, we barely had any junk food available, and we turned out just fine.\n我们小时候几乎没有什么垃圾食品可吃，结果我们也都好好的。\n【信号】时间转换 When we were children。男士用 When we were children 把话题从\"现在的孩\n子\"切回\"自己当年\"，紧跟 we barely had any junk food available。这种\"时间状语引出的\n对比句\"就是过去情况题的答案信号。\n【替换】we barely had any junk food available ↔ They seldom had junk food.\nbarely=seldom 否定频率词\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nThey hardly ate vegetables（几乎不吃蔬菜）把原文\"几乎没有垃圾食品\"的否定对象偷换\n成了\"蔬菜\"。barely/hardly 这个\"几乎不\"的结构是对的，接的名词却被换掉了。\n借用原文：we barely had any junk food available\nC) 张冠李戴\nfavored chocolate-coated sweets（偏爱巧克力糖果）盗用了第7段 chocolate-covered\nsweets，但那是说\"现在广告里的垃圾食品\"，不是说话人小时候爱吃的；时间和主体都安错\n了。\n借用原文：chocolate-covered sweets\nD) 张冠李戴\nliked the food advertised on TV 盗用了前文反复出现的 advertised on TV，但电视广告\n讲的是\"现在的孩子\"，说话人明说自己小时候 barely had any junk food，根本不在电视广\n告的语境里。\n借用原文：advertising on TV", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What can be found in the Valley of the Kings?", "options": ["A) Stretches of farmland.", "B) Typical Egyptian animal farms.", "C) Tombs of ancient rulers.", "D) Ruins left by devastating floods."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Tombs of ancient rulers.\n【定位】A journey through the unobstructed part of this waterway today would pass by the splendid\nValley of the Kings, where the tombs of many of these ancient monarchs have stood for over\n3,000 years.\n今天，沿这条河可通航的河段航行，会经过壮丽的帝王谷，那里矗立着许多古代帝王的陵\n墓，已逾三千年。\n【信号】关键词 Valley of the Kings 后紧跟 where 引出存在物。答案句由专有地名信号 the\nsplendid Valley of the Kings 触发，其后 where the tombs of many of these ancient\nmonarchs have stood for over 3,000 years 用 where 引导出该地存在的事物——陵墓，\n这就是题目问的有什么的落点。\n【替换】the tombs of many of these ancient monarchs ↔ Tombs of ancient rulers.\nmonarchs=rulers 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Stretches of farmland. 盗用了原文 the nearby farmland on which ancient Egypt\ndepended 里的 farmland，但那是讲尼罗河洪水滋养沿岸农田，并不在帝王谷里能找到，\n地点对错位了。\n借用原文：carrying new topsoil to the nearby farmland on which ancient Egypt depended\nB) 未提及\n选项 Typical Egyptian animal farms. 借 Egyptian 与 farm 的关联诱选，原文只提埃及依\n赖农田耕作，从未说帝王谷里有典型的埃及动物农场，属凭埃及二字硬凑、原文无对应内\n容。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Ruins left by devastating floods. 盗用原文 the Nile used to flood annually 的\nflood，但原文洪水是滋养农田的正面作用，根本不是 devastating（毁灭性）的废墟，且\n废墟也不在帝王谷，词对、语义与地点全错。\n借用原文：the Nile used to flood annually, thereby creating, irrigating and carrying new topsoil", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "In what way is the Amazon different from other big rivers?", "options": ["A) It provides habitats for more primitive tribes.", "B) It is hardly associated with great civilizations.", "C) It has not yet been fully explored and exploited.", "D) It gathers water from many tropical rain forests."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is hardly associated with great civilizations.\n【定位】Great civilizations and intensive settlement are hardly associated with the Amazon.\n伟大的文明与密集的人类定居几乎与亚马孙河无缘。\n【信号】段首否定 hardly 标出反常之处。答案句是第 3 段开门第一句，用否定词 Great\ncivilizations and intensive settlement are hardly associated with the Amazon 直接点\n出亚马孙河的反常——别的大河多孕育文明，它却几乎没有，这种段首突出的否定式断言正\n是它与众不同的信号。\n【替换】Great civilizations and intensive settlement are hardly associated with the Amazon ↔\nIt is hardly associated with great civilizations.\n近乎原句保留，仅省去 settlement\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 It provides habitats for more primitive tribes. 里 more primitive tribes（更原始的\n部落）原文从未提及，原文末句只说它 irrigates the largest tropical rain forest on\nearth，是灌溉雨林而非为原始部落提供栖息地，属凭雨林联想硬加。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 It has not yet been fully explored and exploited. 借开发/探索的话题诱选，但原文\n谈的是文明与定居稀少，从未说它尚未被充分开发利用，是把没文明偷换成没开发，原文无\n此层意思。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 It gathers water from many tropical rain forests. 盗用原文 irrigates the largest\ntropical rain forest on earth 的 tropical rain forest，但原文是亚马孙河灌溉雨林（河给\n雨林水），选项却说它从多片雨林汇水（雨林给河水），施受方向正好相反。\n借用原文：the Amazon irrigates the largest tropical rain forest on earth", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the Amazon?", "options": ["A) It carries about one fifth of the world's fresh water.", "B) It has numerous human settlements along its banks.", "C) It is second only to the Mississippi River in width.", "D) It is as long as the Nile and the Yangtze combined."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It carries about one fifth of the world's fresh water.\n【定位】Yet this 4,000-mile-long South American river carries about 20% of the world's fresh water—\nmore than the Mississippi, Nile, and Yangtze combined.\n然而这条长达四千英里的南美河流，携带着全球约百分之二十的淡水——比密西西比河、尼\n罗河和长江加起来还多。\n【信号】数据信号 about 20% 与 more than…combined。答案句以转折 Yet 引出关键数据\ncarries about 20% of the world's fresh water，并补一句 more than the Mississippi,\nNile, and Yangtze combined，含百分比与多于…相加，正是数据题该锁定的统计落点。\n【替换】carries about 20% of the world's fresh water ↔\nIt carries about one fifth of the world's fresh water.\n20%=one fifth 数字换算等值\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项 It has numerous human settlements along its banks. 说沿岸有众多定居点，但原\n文 intensive settlement are hardly associated with the Amazon 恰恰说密集定居几乎与\n它无缘，选项与原文极性相反。\n借用原文：Great civilizations and intensive settlement are hardly associated with the Amazon\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 It is second only to the Mississippi River in width. 盗用原文真念过的 Mississippi\n和 wide，但原文 more than the Mississippi, Nile, and Yangtze combined 是说水量多于\n它们，so wide at some points that…neither shore can be seen 是说它极宽、从未说宽\n度仅次于密西西比河，把不同的量与排名硬凑在一起。\n借用原文：The Amazon is so wide at some points that from its center neither shore can be seen.\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 It is as long as the Nile and the Yangtze combined. 盗用原文 more than the\nMississippi, Nile, and Yangtze combined 里的 Nile、Yangtze、combined，但原文这串\n河名比的是淡水量（carries…more than），不是长度，选项把比较的对象量从水量偷换成\nlong（长度）。\n借用原文：carries about 20% of the world's fresh water—more than the Mississippi, Nile, and\nYangtze combined", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the speaker think is an inevitable truth?", "options": ["A) Living a life in the fast lane leads to success.", "B) We are always in a rush to do various things.", "C) The search for tranquility has become a trend.", "D) All of us actually yearn for a slow and calm life."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） We are always in a rush to do various things.\n【定位】It's an inevitable truth that all of us live a life in the fast lane, even though we know that being\nbusy is not always particularly healthy.\n有一个无可回避的事实：我们所有人都过着快节奏的生活，尽管我们都知道忙碌并不总是那\n么健康。\n【信号】题干词原文复现。信号是题干里的 inevitable truth 在 It's an inevitable truth that all of\nus live a life in the fast lane 中原话出现，that 引导的从句就是这个\"事实\"的具体内容，即\n答案所在。\n【替换】all of us live a life in the fast lane ↔ We are always in a rush to do various things.\n比喻短语转直白同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用了原文里的 fast lane 让人耳熟，但原文只说大家都过快节奏生活，从没说\"快节奏\nleads to success（带来成功）\"，这个因果是选项凭空加上的。\n借用原文：all of us live a life in the fast lane\nC) 答非所问\n\"对平静的追求成了一种潮流\"在原文里并没有作为\"inevitable truth\"被说出；原文提到平静\n（tranquility）是讲她个人的转变和结尾的建议，不是说它已成趋势，本题问的是那个\"事\n实\"，对不上。\nD) 过度推断\n原文说大家\"过着快节奏生活，尽管知道忙不健康\"，并没说\"所有人其实都向往慢而平静的生\n活\"；D 是把说话人结尾的个人主张夸大成\"全体的内心渴望\"，与那句\"事实\"的本意相反。\n借用原文：being busy is not always particularly healthy", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about her life in Tokyo?", "options": ["A) She had trouble balancing family and work.", "B) She enjoyed the various social events.", "C) She was accustomed to tight schedules.", "D) She spent all her leisure time writing books."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She was accustomed to tight schedules.\n【定位】But after I moved to Tokyo and lived there for a number of years, I got used to having a pile\nof to-do lists and my calendar always looked like a mess, with loads of things to do written\nacross it.\n但搬到东京、在那儿住了好些年之后，我习惯了堆成一摞的待办清单，我的日程表总是乱成\n一团，上面密密麻麻写满了要做的事。\n【信号】转折 But 后引出东京生活。信号是段首的转折词 But after I moved to Tokyo——前面讲\n新西兰的慢生活，But 之后才转到东京，这正是本题问的\"东京生活\"落点，紧跟的描述就是\n答案句。\n【替换】I got used to having a pile of to-do lists ↔ She was accustomed to tight schedules.\n短语换近义词 + 具象换概括\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文讲东京生活只说待办多、日程乱、忙得像蜜蜂，从没提\"难以平衡家庭与工作\"；\nbalancing family and work 是文中根本没出现的内容。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 借了原文里的 social events 让人耳熟，但原文是说她被无尽的会议和社交活动\"填满时\n间、忙得团团转\"，是负担，不是\"enjoyed（享受）\"；选项把忙碌偷换成了享受。\n借用原文：filling my time up with endless work meetings and social events, rushing along as busy\nas a bee\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文东京段只讲忙，没说\"闲暇全用来写书\"；book 一词虽在第三段出现，但那是她\"读\n到\"一本书 I came across a book，不是她写书，更不在东京段，D 把无关且改写错的细节\n塞了进来。\n借用原文：I came across a book called In Praise of Slowness", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What made the speaker change her lifestyle?", "options": ["A) The possibility of ruining her family.", "B) Becoming aware of her declining health.", "C) The fatigue from living a fast-paced life.", "D) Reading a book about slowing down."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Reading a book about slowing down.\n【定位】Then, one day, I came across a book called In Praise of Slowness and realized that being busy\nis not only detrimental, but also has the danger of turning life into an endless race.\n后来有一天，我偶然读到一本叫《赞美慢》的书，意识到忙碌不仅有害，还有把生活变成无\n尽赛跑的危险。\n【信号】时间转折 Then, one day 引出起因。信号是 Then, one day 这个时间转折——它标志故事\n从\"一直很忙\"拐向\"开始改变\"，紧跟的\"读到一本书并意识到……\"就是促成改变的那件事，即\n答案。\n【替换】I came across a book called In Praise of Slowness ↔ Reading a book about slowing down.\n动词与书名的同义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文从没说她担心\"忙碌会毁掉家庭\"；ruining her family 是文中完全没有的原因，属凭日\n常想象添加的选项。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说她意识到忙\"detrimental（有害）\"、可能让生活变成 endless race，但这是读书后\n的领悟，并非\"察觉自己健康在下滑\"这一亲身体验；B 把抽象的\"有害\"偷换成具体的\"健康下\n滑\"，且颠倒了\"先读书才醒悟\"的顺序。\n借用原文：being busy is not only detrimental\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 里的快节奏生活确是全篇背景，但原文促成改变的直接起因是\"读到那本书\"，而非\"被快节\n奏累垮\"；选项盗用主题词 fast-paced life 制造耳熟，却把背景错当成了导火索。\n借用原文：rushing along as busy as a bee", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What happened after the speaker changed her lifestyle?", "options": ["A) She started to follow the cultural norms.", "B) She came to enjoy doing everyday tasks.", "C) She learned to use more polite expressions.", "D) She stopped using to-do lists and calendars."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She came to enjoy doing everyday tasks.\n【定位】It doesn't mean that my to-do lists no longer exist, but I've become more aware of the\nimportance of slowing down and making sure that I enjoy the daily activities as I carry\nthem out.\n这并不是说我的待办清单从此不存在了，而是我更意识到放慢节奏的重要，并确保自己在做\n日常事务时是享受其中的。\n【信号】but 后的 I've become 落出真意。信号有两处：前半 It doesn't mean that my to-do\nlists no longer exist 先排除\"完全弃用清单\"这一误解，转折词 but 之后的 I enjoy the\ndaily activities 才是改变后的真实结果，转折后才是落点。\n【替换】I enjoy the daily activities as I carry them out ↔ She came to enjoy doing everyday tasks.\n近义词逐一对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n原文结尾建议大家别用 the cultural norm that says \"busy\"，主张反其道而行，而不是\"开\n始顺从文化习惯\"；A 与原文的主张正好相反。\n借用原文：instead of the cultural norm that says \"busy\"\nC) 未提及\n原文结尾让人改用 exciting and fun 来回答，那是为了体现\"不再以忙为常态\"，不是为\n了\"更礼貌\"；用语更礼貌 polite expressions 是文中没有的说法。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 借了原文真出现的 to-do lists，但原文明确说 It doesn't mean that my to-do lists no\nlonger exist——清单并没有不存在；D 把\"没那么依赖\"夸大成\"彻底停用\"，被前半句直接\n否掉。\n借用原文：It doesn't mean that my to-do lists no longer exist", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What assumption about introduced species is challenged by a number of scientists?", "options": ["A) They will root out native species altogether.", "B) They contribute to a region's biodiversity.", "C) They pose a threat to the local ecosystem.", "D) They will crossbreed with native species."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They pose a threat to the local ecosystem.\n【定位】But a number of scientists question the assumption that the presence of alien species can\nnever be acceptable in a natural ecosystem.\n但有一些科学家质疑这样一个假设：外来物种出现在自然生态系统中永远是不可接受的。\n【信号】转折 But + 题干同词 question the assumption。答案句由转折词 But 领起，且与题干\nassumption 用同一个词 question the assumption，转折后正是被质疑的那条假设；听力\n里 But 之后才是真意，加上题干原词复现，这句必是答案句。\n【替换】the presence of alien species can never be acceptable in a natural ecosystem ↔\nThey pose a threat to the local ecosystem\n永不可接受=被视为对生态的威胁\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第一段确有 root out non-native organisms（铲除外来生物），选项把 root out 搬\n来却改成 root out native species altogether（彻底铲除本地物种）——铲除的对象是外\n来物种、不是本地物种，且 altogether 这种绝对化也无据，是典型听到原词就想选的陷\n阱。\n借用原文：root out non-native organisms\nB) 张冠李戴\n外来物种有益、有助生物多样性是后面科学家反驳时的立场，而非题干要的被质疑的假设；\n选项 contribute to a region's biodiversity 方向正相反，且 biodiversity 一词出现在第五\n段讲威胁时 the greatest threat to biodiversity，借这个词制造熟悉感，实为张冠李戴。\n借用原文：the greatest threat to biodiversity\nD) 未提及\n选项说外来物种会与本地物种 crossbreed with native species（杂交），录音通篇讨论的\n是威胁、控制、归化，从未提到杂交这一点，属凭常识臆造、原文无对应的未提及项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does Dr. Michael Rosenzweig think of exotic and native species?", "options": ["A) Their classifications are meaningful.", "B) Their interactions are hard to define.", "C) Their definitions are changeable.", "D) Their distinctions are artificial."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Their distinctions are artificial.\n【定位】Distinctions between exotic and native species are artificial, said Dr. Michael Rosenzweig, a\nprofessor of evolutionary biology at the University of Arizona, because they depend on\npicking a date and calling the plants and animals that show up after that date exotic.\n外来物种与本地物种之间的区分是人为的，亚利桑那大学进化生物学教授 Michael\nRosenzweig 博士说，因为这种区分取决于挑一个日期、把此后出现的动植物称作外来\n物种。\n【信号】人名 said Dr. Rosenzweig 直接领出本人观点。答案句中 said Dr. Michael Rosenzweig\n把发言人锁定为题干问的那个人，他亲口给出的判断 Distinctions between exotic and\nnative species are artificial 就是观点句；态度题紧跟人名找他说的评价句即可定位。\n【替换】Distinctions between exotic and native species are artificial ↔ Their distinctions are artificial\n几乎原句压缩，artificial 评价一致\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nRosenzweig 明说区分是 artificial（人为、不真实），选项却说分类 Their classifications\nare meaningful（有意义、站得住），与他的立场完全相反，是把观点极性反转的陷阱。\n借用原文：Distinctions between exotic and native species are artificial\nB) 偷换概念\n选项说它们的 interactions are hard to define（相互作用难界定），但原文谈的是\ndistinctions（区分）人为，且说难以界定的不是 interactions；选项借 species 的话题感\n把 distinction 偷换成 interaction，答非所问。\n借用原文：Distinctions between exotic and native species are artificial\nC) 过度推断\n选项说 Their definitions are changeable（定义可变），原文虽提到区分取决于 picking a\ndate（挑个日期），但 Rosenzweig 的核心判断是这条区分本身人为，而非强调定义会\n变；changeable 看似沾边，却不是他给出的定性，属偏离重心。\n借用原文：picking a date and calling the plants and animals that show up after that date exotic", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does Professor Don Smith say about alien species?", "options": ["A) Only a few of them cause problems to native species.", "B) They may turn out to benefit the local environment.", "C) Few of them can survive in their new habitats.", "D) Only 10 percent of them can be naturalized."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Only a few of them cause problems to native species.\n【定位】Only a small percentage of alien species cause problems in their new habitats, said Don\nSmith, professor of ecology and evolutionary biology at the University of Tennessee.\n只有一小部分外来物种会在新栖息地造成问题，田纳西大学生态与进化生物学教授 Don\nSmith 说。\n【信号】人名 said Don Smith + 数量限定 Only a small percentage。答案句由 said Don Smith\n锁定发言人，开头的数量限定 Only a small percentage 正是他给出的核心事实；题干问某\n人 say about，紧跟人名的这句限定数量的陈述就是答案句。\n【替换】Only a small percentage of alien species cause problems in their new habitats ↔\nOnly a few of them cause problems to native species\na small percentage = a few，同义换说\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项说外来物种 may turn out to benefit the local environment（可能有益于本地环\n境），但原文末尾 Smith 的真意恰相反——他提醒 non-native species can appear\nharmless for decades, then turn invasive（看似无害多年后会转为入侵），强调的是别被\n表象骗，而非可能有益；选项把警示反向曲解。\n借用原文：non-native species can appear harmless for decades, then turn invasive\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Few of them can survive in their new habitats（少有能存活）借用了原文 in their\nnew habitats 这一原词，但 Smith 说的是少数会惹问题、九成已 fit into their\nenvironments 适应良好，恰恰是大多数能存活；选项把适应说成不能存活，与原文相反。\n借用原文：fit into their environments and are considered naturalized\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 only about 10 percent are invasive——10% 是入侵的比例；选项 Only 10\npercent of them can be naturalized 把数字搬对，却说成只有 10% 能归化，与原文 The\nother 90 percent…are considered naturalized（九成才是归化）正好颠倒，是典型听到\n数字就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：only about 10 percent are invasive", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What should you do when doing business with foreigners?", "options": ["A) Respect their traditional culture.", "B) Attend their business seminars.", "C) Research their specific demands.", "D) Adopt the right business strategies."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Respect their traditional culture.\n【定位】It is only polite for us to do the same when we visit them. It is not only polite. It is essential if\nwe want to sell British products overseas.\n我们去拜访他们时，做到同样这一点才算有礼貌。这还不只是礼貌问题，如果我们想把英国\n产品卖到海外，这是必不可少的。\n【信号】强调句 It is essential 标出讲者的核心建议。讲者先说对方卖给我们时会努力说英语、尊重\n我们的传统方法，紧接着用 It is only polite for us to do the same 要求我们反过来也这\n样，并用 It is essential if we want to sell British products overseas 加重语气——\n“essential（必不可少）”这种强调用词，就是答案句的信号。\n【替换】respect our traditions and methods ↔ Respect their traditional culture\ntraditions and methods 概括成 traditional culture\ndo the same when we visit them ↔ Respect their traditional culture.\ndo the same 指代前句的“尊重对方传统”\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音开头有 our series of business seminars，选项 Attend their business seminars. 正\n是盗用了 seminars 这个原词。但“seminars”指的是讲者自己办的这场系列讲座，根本不是\n“和外国人做生意该做什么”的答案，纯靠耳熟骗选。\n借用原文：our series of business seminars\nC) 未提及\nResearch their specific demands.（研究他们的具体需求）听起来像很专业的商务建议，\n但录音里讲者给的总纲是“尊重对方传统”，全程没有讲“研究具体需求”。这是把一个看似合\n理的商务套话强加进来，原文未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\nAdopt the right business strategies.（采取正确的商业策略）是个很笼统的大词，看似稳\n妥。但讲者明确给的建议是“尊重对方传统方法”这一具体点，而非泛泛的“正确策略”；用一\n个更大更空的概念把原文的具体主张换掉了。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What must you avoid doing with your Indian colleague?", "options": ["A) Showing them your palm.", "B) Giving them gifts of great value.", "C) Drinking alcohol on certain days of a month.", "D) Clicking your fingers loudly in their presence."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Drinking alcohol on certain days of a month.\n【定位】So No. 1, you must not drink alcohol on the first and seventh of the month in India.\n那么第一题，在印度，每月的一号和七号你绝不能喝酒。\n【信号】禁止语气 must not + 国家名 India 双重信号。这句同时出现 you must not drink\nalcohol 的强禁止语气和定位锚 in India，问“必须避免做什么 + 印度同事”，正好对上\nmust not 和 India 两个信号，定位非常稳。\n【替换】drink alcohol on the first and seventh of the month ↔\nDrinking alcohol on certain days of a month\nthe first and seventh 概括成 certain days\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nShowing them your palm. 里的“手掌 palm”来自录音讲泰国如何叫服务员那段：Place\nyour palm downward and make an inconspicuous waving gesture。那是泰国的“正确\n做法”，不是印度的禁忌，被搬到印度题里张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Place your palm downward and make an inconspicuous waving gesture\nB) 张冠李戴\nGiving them gifts of great value. 里的“贵重礼物”取自讲阿拉伯国家那段 presenting him\nwith a gift of equal worth and beauty。那是阿拉伯礼俗，且并非“必须避免”，被错挂到\n印度同事头上。\n借用原文：presenting him with a gift of equal worth and beauty\nD) 张冠李戴\nClicking your fingers loudly in their presence. 来自泰国段 In Thailand, clicking the\nfingers, clapping your hands, or just shouting \"Waiter!\", will embarrass your hosts。\n这是泰国叫服务员的禁忌，不是印度同事的禁忌，国家对错了。\n借用原文：In Thailand, clicking the fingers, clapping your hands, or just shouting \"Waiter!\", will\nembarrass your hosts", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do we learn about some Spanish business people?", "options": ["A) They are very easy to satisfy.", "B) They have a strong sense of worth.", "C) They tend to be friendly and enthusiastic.", "D) They have a break from 2:00 to 5:30 p.m."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They have a break from 2:00 to 5:30 p.m.\n【定位】And finally, in Spain, some businesses maintain the pattern of working until about 2 o'clock\nand then returning to the office from 5:30 to 8:00, 9:00 or 10:00 in the evening.\n最后，在西班牙，有些企业保持这样的作息：工作到大约两点，然后从下午五点半再回办公\n室，一直工作到晚上八点、九点或十点。\n【信号】国家名 Spain + 数字时间是定位与答案信号。in Spain 是定位锚，紧跟的数字 working\nuntil about 2 o'clock and then returning to the office from 5:30 是答案承载点——听\n到国家名加一串时间数字，就是本题的落点。\n【替换】working until about 2 o'clock and then returning to the office from 5:30 ↔\nThey have a break from 2:00 to 5:30 p.m.\n“干到2点、5点半再回”推出“2点到5点半休息”\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are very easy to satisfy.（很容易满足）录音里关于西班牙人压根没说过，属于自己\n脑补的性格评价，原文只讲了作息时间，没有任何关于“是否容易满足”的内容。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey have a strong sense of worth. 里的“worth（价值）”呼应的是阿拉伯段 a gift of\nequal worth and beauty 中的 worth，被移植成对西班牙人的性格判断。原文讲西班牙只\n说了作息，没有这种评价。\n借用原文：a gift of equal worth and beauty\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey tend to be friendly and enthusiastic.（友好热情）对应的是录音讲阿拉伯人那段\nthe politeness and generosity of the people are without parallel。这是阿拉伯人的特\n点，被张冠李戴给了西班牙人。\n借用原文：the politeness and generosity of the people are without parallel", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What did George Grune do in 1984?", "options": ["A) He completely changed the company's culture.", "B) He collected paintings by world-famous artists.", "C) He took over the sales department of Reader's Digest.", "D) He had the company's boardroom extensively renovated."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He completely changed the company's culture.\n【定位】Shortly after he took over the Reader's Digest Association in 1984, George Grune unlocked\nthe company's boardroom and announced that the room was now open to the employees. It\nwas a symbolic act... True to his word, Grune has shaken up the culture here.\n在 1984 年接掌《读者文摘》协会后不久,乔治·格鲁内打开了公司董事会议室的门锁,宣布这\n个房间现在向全体员工开放。这是一个象征性举动……正如他所言,格鲁内彻底搅动了这里的\n文化。\n【信号】时间锚点 1984 加结论句。录音里 Shortly after he took over the Reader's Digest\nAssociation in 1984 出现题干同款时间 1984,顺着往下,真正的结论是 Grune has shaken\nup the culture here——开锁只是象征性动作,搅动文化才是这件事的实质。\n【替换】Grune has shaken up the culture here ↔ completely changed the company's culture\nshaken up=completely changed\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到董事会议室里有 paintings by many world-famous artists like Monet and\nPicasso,但那是介绍公司文化时举的例子,说的是公司收藏,并非 Grune 在 1984 做的事;选项\ncollected paintings by world-famous artists 把听到的名画原词搬来安到 Grune 头上。\n借用原文：paintings by many world-famous artists like Monet and Picasso\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说他 took over the Reader's Digest Association in 1984,是接掌整个协会;选项 took\nover the sales department of Reader's Digest 盗用 took over 这个原词,却把接掌对象偷\n偷缩小成销售部门,与原文不符。\n借用原文：took over the Reader's Digest Association in 1984\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里他对董事会议室做的是 unlocked the company's boardroom 并对员工开放,这是个\n象征性举动;选项 had the company's boardroom extensively renovated 盗用\nboardroom 原词,却把开锁开放篡改成大修翻新,动作完全两样。\n借用原文：unlocked the company's boardroom", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "How did the Wallaces define the formula for Reader's Digest?", "options": ["A) It should be sold at a reasonable price.", "B) Its articles should be short and inspiring.", "C) It should be published in the world's leading languages.", "D) Its articles should entertain blue- and pink-collar workers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Its articles should be short and inspiring.\n【定位】The Wallaces... had a clearly defined formula for their \"little magazine\"... Articles were to be\nshort, readable and uplifting. Subjects were picked to inspire or entertain.\n华莱士夫妇……为他们这本最初副标题叫小杂志的刊物定下了清晰的公式……文章要短小、好\n读、催人向上。选题旨在启发或娱乐读者。\n【信号】formula 后的列举即定义。录音先说 had a clearly defined formula,紧接着用 Articles\nwere to be short, readable and uplifting 把公式逐条列出,were to be 这种规定式语气标\n志着这就是他们定下的办刊要点,是答案句。\n【替换】Articles were to be short, readable and uplifting ↔ Its articles should be short and inspiring\nwere to be=should;uplifting=inspiring\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从未说华莱士给杂志定价合理;选项 It should be sold at a reasonable price. 是无中生\n有,录音讲公式时只谈文章与选题,未涉定价。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 selling 28 million copies each month in 17 languages 提到 languages,但那是讲\n杂志后来的发行规模,不是华莱士定的公式;选项 It should be published in the world's\nleading languages. 把 languages 原词搬到办刊主张上,张冠李戴。\n借用原文：selling 28 million copies each month in 17 languages\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说选题 Subjects were picked to inspire or entertain,entertain 是面向读者的;blue-\nand pink-collar 则出现在讲后来裁员的第三段;选项 Its articles should entertain blue-\nand pink-collar workers. 把两处不相干的原词 entertain 和 blue- and pink-collar 拼在一\n起,制造像对的假象。\n借用原文：Subjects were picked to inspire or entertain", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about the founder of Reader's Digest, DeWitt Wallace?", "options": ["A) He knew how to make the magazine profitable.", "B) He served as a church minister for many years.", "C) He suffered many setbacks and misfortunes in his life.", "D) He treated the employees like members of his family."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He treated the employees like members of his family.\n【定位】It started as a mom-and-pop operation and the childless Wallaces always considered\nemployees to be part of their family. Employees still tell stories of how the Wallaces would\ntake care of their employees who had met with misfortunes...\n公司起步时是夫妻店,没有子女的华莱士夫妇始终把员工视为家庭的一员。至今员工还在讲述\n华莱士夫妇如何照顾那些遭遇不幸的员工……\n【信号】明确陈述待员工如家人。录音直接陈述 the childless Wallaces always considered\nemployees to be part of their family,这是对创始人待人风格的正面定性句,always 加重了\n语气,正是答案句。\n【替换】considered employees to be part of their family ↔\ntreated the employees like members of his family\nconsider as part of=treat like member\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n懂得让杂志盈利、盯利润的是后来的 Grune(has his eye focused on the bottom line),不\n是创始人 Wallace;选项 He knew how to make the magazine profitable. 把 Grune 的特\n征安到创始人头上,张冠李戴。\n借用原文：has his eye focused on the bottom line\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说华莱士夫妇是 both children of church ministers,即他们是牧师的孩子,本人并非牧\n师;选项 He served as a church minister for many years. 盗用 church minister 原词,却把\n身份从父母错挂到创始人本人。\n借用原文：both children of church ministers\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说华莱士夫妇照顾那些 employees who had met with misfortunes,遭遇不幸的是员\n工、创始人是去照顾他们;选项 He suffered many setbacks and misfortunes in his life.\n盗用 misfortunes 原词,却把承受不幸的人由员工偷换成创始人本人。\n借用原文：employees who had met with misfortunes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What change took place in Reader's Digest after the Wallaces' death?", "options": ["A) It carried many more advertisements.", "B) George Grune joined it as an ad salesman.", "C) Several hundred of its employees got fired.", "D) Its subscriptions increased considerably."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Several hundred of its employees got fired.\n【定位】George Grune... turned the magazine on its head. He laid off several hundred\nworkers. Especially hard hit were the blue- and pink-collar departments such as\nsubscription fulfillment.\n乔治·格鲁内……把这本杂志彻底翻了个底朝天。他裁掉了数百名员工。受冲击最重的是蓝领\n和粉领部门,比如订阅履行部门。\n【信号】转变后明说裁员数百。第三段先用 turned the magazine on its head 点出巨变,随即明说\nHe laid off several hundred workers,laid off several hundred 是对裁员这一变化的直接\n陈述,是答案句。\n【替换】He laid off several hundred workers ↔ Several hundred of its employees got fired\nlaid off=got fired;workers=employees\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音讲华莱士时代提过广告 didn't accept advertising in the US edition until 1955,但那\n是早年情况,并非夫妇去世后的变化;选项 It carried many more advertisements. 借广告这\n一话题无中生有,录音没说后来广告增多。\n借用原文：didn't accept advertising in the US edition until 1955\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说 Grune 是 a former ad salesman who joined Reader's Digest in 1960,他 1960\n年就入职,远在华莱士夫妇 1980 年代初去世之前,不是去世后的变化;选项 George Grune\njoined it as an ad salesman. 盗用 ad salesman、joined 原词,却把它当成去世后发生的\n事。\n借用原文：a former ad salesman who joined Reader's Digest in 1960\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说订阅相关部门是受冲击重灾区 Especially hard hit were the blue- and pink-collar\ndepartments such as subscription fulfillment,订阅部门是被裁、被打击,而非订阅增长;选\n项 Its subscriptions increased considerably. 盗用 subscription 原词,却把方向由受冲击\n反转成大增。\n借用原文：Especially hard hit were the blue- and pink-collar departments such as\nsubscription fulfillment", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) reputed\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 woman 后修饰，且后接 for 引导短语\n【依据】「被认为(创作了它)的那位女性」\nbe reputed for / reputed for 表「以……著称、被认为(做了)……」，与前文 Did Sarah\nJosepha Hale write “Mary’s Little Lamb” ... This is still disputed 的「作者归属存疑」氛\n围完全契合——她是「被认为创作了这首诗」的女性。\n【竞争词】M) supposed 搭配偏好不同 + 留给 28 题更贴\nsupposed 多用作前置定语（如 the supposed author）表「所谓的、据称的」，与 for\nwriting it 的后置搭配不及 reputed 自然；而 28 题正缺一个修饰 author 的前置形容词，\nsupposed author 在此搭配更紧。坚持 M 反而占掉 28 题的最佳答案。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— supposed 与 reputed 义近，易抢答 M。考生若按词义\n「两者都通」匆忙选 M，会同时挤掉 28 题最合适的 supposed。防坑法：看句法位置——\n后置 + for 短语用 reputed，前置定语用 supposed。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) characters\n名词\n【词性槽】America’s most fascinating ___ 中所有格 + 形容词最高级后缺名词，且需复数\n【依据】「人物」才与「woman/fascinating」对位\n前文 the woman __(26)__ for writing it was one of America’s most fascinating __(27)__\n——主语是 the woman（人），one of 之后的复数名词指代同类（人），character 表\n「人物」恰合：「美国最迷人的人物之一」。\n【竞争词】D) features 对象错位\nfeatures 表「特征/特色」或「专题报道」，描述的是「事物/抽象属性」而非「人」；与主语\nthe woman 的同类指代不能对位。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) supposed\n形容词\n【词性槽】the 与名词 author 之间的前置定语位\n【依据】「所谓的/据称的」作者——与争议主题呼应\n首句 Did Sarah Josepha Hale write “Mary’s Little Lamb” ... This is still disputed 已把\n「是否真为作者」立成悬念，此处再说 the __(28)__ author’s life 用 supposed 表「据称的\n作者」在语义上严丝合缝；the supposed author 是英文常见搭配。\n【竞争词】G) latter 需对照项\nlatter 必须有「前者/后者」两项可对照，本句只提一人，搭配不成立。\nO) versatile 信息越界\nversatile 强调「多才多艺/多领域」，本段只在引入「作者生平」，尚未铺陈她的多面身份，把\n判断前置到这里属于过度推断；且要留给 29 题描述她「作家 + 社会倡导者」的多重身份。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— versatile author 看似合理，实为越界。the versatile\nauthor 形态正确、义看似通，但段落定调是「作者存疑」，应选 supposed。防坑法：让\n形容词色彩与本段首句的定调词对齐，再下笔。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) fierce\n形容词\n【词性槽】a 与名词 social advocate 之间作前置定语\n【依据】「强势/积极进取」的社会倡导者\n下一段交代 17 years of advocacy including letters to five presidents 的执着——这种坚\n定不移、激进游说的形象，正是 a fierce advocate（强势的倡导者）这一固定搭配的典型\n语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) obsessed\n形容词\n【词性槽】was 之后、紧跟 with 短语，需作表语的过去分词形容词\n【依据】be obsessed with 是固定搭配\nshe was particularly __(30)__ with an ideal New England, which she associated with\nabundant Thanksgiving meals——下文用 abundant Thanksgiving meals 描绘她对「理\n想新英格兰」的痴迷，be obsessed with 表「痴迷于」恰好对位。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) campaign\n名词\n【词性槽】begin 的宾语位置，前有 a 与形容词 nationwide\n【依据】begin a nationwide campaign 是固定搭配\nShe began a nationwide __(31)__ to have a national holiday declared 后文紧跟 17\nyears of advocacy including letters to five presidents，正是「发起一场全国性的游说运\n动」。begin/launch a campaign 是高频搭配。\n【竞争词】B) career 义偏宽泛\ncareer 指「职业生涯」，与「为推动某节日设立而展开的具体运动」不对位，且无法与\nnationwide 自然搭配。\nC) characters 复数且义不合\ncharacters 复数，与 a ___ 的单数框架冲突；「人物」也无法用 nationwide 修饰。\nL) rest 义不搭\nrest 表「剩余/休息」，与「全国性运动」语义不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) traditional\n形容词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 与名词 festivals 之间，缺定语\n【依据】与「感恩节」语境的「惯例节日」最贴\n上文交代 Hale 把理想的新英格兰与 abundant Thanksgiving meals 联系起来，再去\nbring families together while celebrating the __(32)__ festivals——感恩节正是美国\n「传统节日」，traditional festivals 是高频固定搭配。\n【竞争词】E) fierce 感情色彩冲突\nfierce 表「激烈/凶猛」，与「家人团聚庆祝节日」的温情语境正相反，不能修饰 festivals。\nG) latter 需「前后两者」对照才能用\nlatter 通常指此前提到过的两者中的「后者」，本句并无两个对照项，语境不成立。\nO) versatile 义不搭\nversatile 多才多艺/多用途，用来形容人或工具，搭配 festivals 不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) proclamation\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 issued 后缺宾语，且前有不定冠词 a\n【依据】总统发布的「公告/宣言」\n总统 issued a __(33)__ setting aside the last Thursday in November for the holiday 描\n述的正是林肯于 1863 年发布「感恩节公告」这一历史事实，issue a proclamation 是固定\n搭配：「发布公告」。\n【竞争词】B) career 语义不通\nissue a career 不成立，「职业」与「指定某天为节日」逻辑无关。\nC) characters 单复数与词义双不合\ncharacters 复数，与 a ___ 的单数框架矛盾；且「人物/角色」无法与 issue 及「设节日」逻辑连\n起来。\nL) rest 义不搭\nissue a rest 不成立；rest 在此意为「剩余/休息」，与发布正式文件无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) inspired\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 was 之后、by 引出施事，被动语态需过去分词\n【依据】「由真实事件激发」才合逻辑\n下一句 When young Mary Sawyer was followed to school by a lamb in 1816, it\ncaused some problems 正是给出「真实事件」的具体内容——一只小羊跟着玛丽去上\n学。这正是「这首诗由一桩真实事件所启发」。be inspired by 是固定搭配。\n【竞争词】J) rectified 逻辑指向反\nrectify 意为「纠正错误」，「诗被一桩真实事件纠正」于义不通，且 be rectified by 在文学创\n作语境中不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) campaign", "B) career", "C) characters", "D) features", "E) fierce", "F) inspired", "G) latter", "H) obsessed", "I) proclamation", "J) rectified", "K) reputed", "L) rest", "M) supposed", "N) traditional", "O) versatile"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) rest\n名词\n【词性槽】for the ___ of her life 介词短语中、of 前缺名词\n【依据】for the rest of her life 是固定短语\nHale claimed for the __(35)__ of her life that “some other people pretended that\nsomeone else wrote the poem”——for the rest of her life 意为「在余生中」，正合\n「她终生都坚称作品是自己写的」这一语义。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "There are a couple of plants tough and adaptable enough to survive on bare rocky hills and in deserts.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】光秃岩山与沙漠上少数顽强植物\n【定位】Yet on these and similar formations in deserts around the world, a few fierce plants have\nadapted to endure under ever-changing conditions.\n然而在这些地貌以及世界各地沙漠中类似的地貌上，少数几种顽强的植物已经适应了不断变\n化的环境、得以存活下去。\n【改写】tough and adaptable enough to survive ↔ a few fierce plants have adapted to endure\n形容词与动词的同义对\nbare rocky hills and in deserts ↔ rocky hills\n画面名词原样复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Farrant is trying to isolate genes in resurrection plants and reproduce them in crops.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】分离复苏植物基因并在作物里复现\n【定位】After completing her Ph. D. on seeds, Farrant began investigating whether it might be\npossible to isolate the properties that make most seeds so resilient and transfer them to other\nplant tissues.\n在完成关于种子的博士论文之后，Farrant 开始研究：是否有可能把让大多数种子如此耐受\n的性状分离出来，再转移到植物的其他组织里。\n【改写】isolate genes in resurrection plants ↔ isolate the properties that make most seeds so resilient\n上位词 properties 在同段被收束到 genes\nreproduce them in crops ↔ transfer them to other plant tissues\ntissues→crops 的下位具化 + transfer→reproduce 的近义\n【辨邻】 I I 段说I'm trying three methods of breeding...conventional, genetic modification\nand gene editing——这是已经分离完基因之后的“用哪种方法把它放进作物”的环\n节，而题干问的是isolate genes这个分离动作本身，分离在 H 段完成。\nI'm trying three methods of breeding...conventional, genetic modification and gene editing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Farmers in South Africa are more at the mercy of nature, especially inconsistent rainfall.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】南非农民受降雨摆布\n【定位】Food security and fortunes depend on sufficient rain, and nowhere more so than in\nAfrica, where 96% of farmland depends on rain instead of the irrigation common in more\ndeveloped places.\n粮食安全与家庭收成都系于充沛的降雨，而在这一点上最甚的莫过于非洲——那里 96% 的\n农田都靠天吃饭，而不像更发达的地区那样普遍依赖灌溉。\n【改写】Farmers in South Africa are more at the mercy of nature ↔ nowhere more so than in Africa\n受自然支配 ↔ 比较级强调\nespecially inconsistent rainfall ↔ 96% of farmland depends on rain instead of the irrigation\n不可预测的降雨 ↔ 靠雨吃饭的数据\n【辨邻】 K K 段提及a drought like the present one in South Africa时只是顺带说明目标用户\n群，主旨是产量和服务对象，并未陈述“南非农民总体受降雨摆布”这件事；这件事是 A\n段开篇的总论。\nsubsistence farmers who need help to survive a drought like the present one in South Africa", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Resurrection crops are most likely to be the choice of subsistence farmers.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】复苏作物面向自给型农民\n【定位】\"The yield is never going to be high,\" Farrant says, so these plants will be targeted not at\nIowa farmers trying to squeeze more cash out of high-yield fields, but subsistence farmers\nwho need help to survive a drought like the present one in South Africa.\nFarrant 说：“产量永远不会高”，所以这些植物的目标用户不是想从高产田里再多榨些收入\n的爱荷华农民，而是那些需要帮忙才能扛过像南非眼下这种旱灾的自给型农民。\n【改写】Resurrection crops ↔ these plants\n代词指代上文“复苏作物”\nmost likely to be the choice of subsistence farmers ↔\nthese plants will be targeted not at Iowa farmers trying to squeeze more cash out of high-yield\nfields, but subsistence farmers\n目标人群对照结构 ↔ 倾向性表达", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Even though many plants have developed various tactics to cope with dry weather, they cannot survive a prolonged drought.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】各种耐旱策略仍熬不过持久干旱\n【定位】But once these plants use up their stored reserve or tap out the underground supply, they cease\ngrowing and start to die.\n但是一旦这些植物用光了储存的水分、或把地下水抽尽了，它们就停止生长、开始枯死。\n【改写】many plants have developed various tactics to cope with dry weather ↔\nMany different kinds of plants have developed tactics to weather dry spells\n几乎原样的让步前半句\nthey cannot survive a prolonged drought ↔\nonce these plants use up their stored reserve or tap out the underground supply, they cease\ngrowing and start to die\n结果同义重述：长期断水 = 终究会死\n【辨邻】 F F 段写复苏植物recover from months and years without water——能撑过长期干\n旱；陈述句的结论是cannot survive a prolonged drought——撑不过。结论方向相\n反，所以不是源头段。\nThey can recover from months and years without water, depending on the species", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Despite consumer resistance, researchers are pushing ahead with genetic modification of crops.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】消费者抗拒下仍推进转基因\n【定位】She says she is aware that plenty of people do not want to eat genetically modified crops, but\nshe is pushing ahead with every available tool until one works.\n她说，自己很清楚有很多人不愿意吃转基因作物，但她仍在用一切可用的手段全力推进，直\n到有一种方法成功为止。\n【改写】Despite consumer resistance ↔\nshe is aware that plenty of people do not want to eat genetically modified crops\n让步从句 ↔ “承认但仍推进”句式\npushing ahead with genetic modification of crops ↔\nshe is pushing ahead with every available tool until one works\n动词原样复现 + 宾语收束", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Most seeds can pull through dry spells and begin growing when conditions are ripe, but once this process starts, it cannot be held back.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】种子能熬过旱季，发芽后再也按不下暂停键\n【定位】Most seeds can wait out the dry, unwelcoming seasons until conditions are right and they\nsprout. Yet once they start growing, such plants seem not to retain the ability to hit the pause\nbutton on metabolism in their stems or leaves.\n大多数种子可以熬过干燥而不友好的季节，直到条件合适才发芽。但一旦它们开始生长，这\n类植物似乎就再也没法在茎或叶的代谢上按下暂停键。\n【改写】Most seeds can pull through dry spells and begin growing when conditions are ripe ↔\nMost seeds can wait out the dry, unwelcoming seasons until conditions are right and they\nsprout\n动词同义 + 时间从句结构\nonce this process starts, it cannot be held back ↔\nonce they start growing, such plants seem not to retain the ability to hit the pause button on\nmetabolism in their stems or leaves\n时间从句 + 比喻同义\n【辨邻】 F F 段主语是已生长的Resurrection plants，讲的是它们如何减慢代谢；题干主语是\nMost seeds，对应的是 G 段那种“种子—发芽—无法暂停”的链条。\nThat slows the plant's metabolism and protects its dried-out tissues", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Farrant is working hard to cultivate food crops that can survive extreme dryness by studying the traits of rare wild plants.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】通过研究稀有野生植物特性来培育耐旱作物\n【定位】She is hard at work finding a way to take traits from rare wild plants that adapt to extreme dry\nweather and use them in food crops.\n她正在努力寻找方法，把适应极端干燥环境的稀有野生植物身上的性状取出来，用到粮食作\n物里。\n【改写】cultivate food crops that can survive extreme dryness ↔\ntake traits from rare wild plants that adapt to extreme dry weather and use them in food crops\n目标动作链 ↔ 概括动词\nby studying the traits of rare wild plants ↔ take traits from rare wild plants\n方法状语 ↔ 取材动作\n【辨邻】 H H 段的具体动作是isolate the properties that make most seeds so resilient and\ntransfer them to other plant tissues，已经是技术细节；题干cultivate food\ncrops...by studying the traits of rare wild plants是总目标宣言，对应 B 段的整段总\n目标take traits from rare wild plants that adapt to extreme dry weather and use\nthem in food crops。\nisolate the properties that make most seeds so resilient and transfer them to other plant tissues", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "By adjusting their metabolism, resurrection plants can recover from an extended period of drought.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】靠调整代谢从长期干旱中恢复\n【定位】They lack water-storing structures, and their existence on rock faces prevents them from\ntapping groundwater, so they have instead developed the ability to change their metabolism.\n... They can recover from months and years without water, depending on the species.\n它们没有储水结构，又因为生长在岩面上而无法汲取地下水，所以它们转而进化出了改变自\n身代谢的能力。……根据物种不同，它们能在断水数月乃至数年后恢复生机。\n【改写】By adjusting their metabolism ↔ developed the ability to change their metabolism\n动词同义\nresurrection plants can recover from an extended period of drought ↔\nThey can recover from months and years without water\n抽象时间 ↔ 具体时间\n【辨邻】 D D 段时间尺度是in a matter of hours，本题问的是extended period of drought“长\n期干旱”，时间尺度对不上；F 段recover from months and years without water才\n匹配。\nBut rainfall can revive them in a matter of hours\nE E 段主语是these plants即耐旱植物，结论是they cease growing and start to die“死\n亡”，与题干“复苏植物 + 恢复”主语和结论都不一致。\nonce these plants use up their stored reserve or tap out the underground supply, they cease growing\nand start to die", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Resurrection plants can come back to life in a short time after a rainfall.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】雨后短时间内复活\n【定位】But rainfall can revive them in a matter of hours.\n但是降雨可以在几小时之内让它们复活。\n【改写】Resurrection plants can come back to life ↔ rainfall can revive them\n动词同义 + 主被动转换\nin a short time after a rainfall ↔ in a matter of hours\n抽象短时间 ↔ 具体几小时\n【辨邻】 F F 段写recover from months and years without water——长期复苏；本题问in a\nshort time——短时间复活，时间尺度不一致。\nThey can recover from months and years without water, depending on the species", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Compared with human memory, machines can ________.", "options": ["A) Identify human faces more efficiently", "B) Tell a friend from a mere acquaintance", "C) Store an unlimited number of human faces", "D) Perceive images invisible to the human eye"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Compared with human memory, machines can ________. 是一个待补全的客观陈述\n句,无 infer/imply/mainly 等推断/主旨标志,属于对原文某句的客观转述。\n【拆句】Compared with human memory, machines can ________. 是分词短语作状语+主句的简\n单结构。Compared with human memory 是过去分词短语作状语,逻辑主语是后面的\nmachines,意为\"与人类记忆相比,机器可以……\"。无陷阱。\n【定位】Machines aren't limited this way. Give the right computer a massive database of faces, and\nit can process what it sees—then recognize a face it's told to find—with remarkable speed\nand precision.\n机器不受这种限制。给一台合适的电脑一个海量人脸数据库,它就能处理所看到的内容——\n然后以惊人的速度和精度识别出被要求查找的人脸。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwith remarkable speed and precision ↔ more efficiently\nMachines aren't limited this way. ↔ identify human faces\n预判的\"更高效地识别人脸\"与 identify human faces more efficiently 在动作(识别人\n脸)和评价(more efficiently=快+准)上完全等值,锁定 A。\nB) 无中生有\ntell a friend from a mere acquaintance 把\"区分朋友与一般熟人\"嫁接到机器身上,而\nbased on the number of acquaintances 只是用 acquaintances 估算\"人类能记多少\n张脸\"的样本,从未说机器能区分\"朋友 vs 熟人\"。\nC) 过度推断\nstore an unlimited number of human faces 中 unlimited(无上限)是绝对化推断;原文\n只说 Machines aren't limited this way.——\"不像人类那样受限\",并未说\"无上限\";P5 反\n而显示规模一大、准确率就下降,印证机器并非真正 unlimited。\nD) 无中生有\nperceive images invisible to the human eye\"识别人眼看不见的图像\"是另一种科幻能\n力,原文通篇只讨论\"快/准/规模\",从未提到\"人眼可见 vs 不可见\"这一维度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why did researchers create Mega Face?", "options": ["A) To enlarge the volume of the facial-recognition database.", "B) To increase the variety of facial-recognition software.", "C) To understand computers' problems with facial recognition.", "D) To reduce the complexity of facial-recognition algorithms."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why did researchers create Mega Face? 问的是具体行为的具体原因,答案就在原文\n目的状语里,不需要跨段推理。\n【拆句】Why did researchers create Mega Face? 是典型 Why 一般疑问句,主语 researchers、谓\n语 did create、宾语 Mega Face。无陷阱。\n【定位】To begin to figure out how computers are struggling, researchers at the University of\nWashington created a massive database of faces—they call it MegaFace—and tested a variety\nof facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in complexity.\n为了开始搞清楚电脑在哪些方面陷入挣扎,华盛顿大学的研究者们建立了一个海量人脸数据库\n——他们称之为 MegaFace——并测试了多种面部识别算法随复杂度变化的表现。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTo enlarge the volume of the facial-recognition database.\"扩大数据库容量\"是研究\n者达成目的的\"手段\",不是\"目的\";原文 created a massive database of faces 描述的就\n是这一手段,而真正的目的在前置不定式里。\nB) 偷换概念\nTo increase the variety of facial-recognition software. 把\"测试多种算法\"偷换成\"增\n加软件种类\";原文 tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms 是\"测试\"而非\"增\n加\",且测试对象是 algorithms(算法)不是 software(软件)。\nC) ✓ 正确\nfigure out how computers are struggling ↔\nTo understand computers' problems with facial recognition.\ntested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms ↔ with facial recognition\n预判的\"搞清电脑在面部识别上的问题\"几乎逐词对应 To understand computers'\nproblems with facial recognition.,锁定 C。\nD) 与原文相反\nTo reduce the complexity of facial-recognition algorithms.\"降低算法复杂度\"与原文\n相反:tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms(算法) as they scaled up in\ncomplexity 说的是\"算法复杂度逐步上升\",研究者是\"伴随复杂度上升测试它们\",不是\"降\n低复杂度\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the passage say about machine accuracy?", "options": ["A) It falls short of researchers' expectations.", "B) It improves with added computing power.", "C) It varies greatly with different algorithms.", "D) It decreases as the database size increases."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about machine accuracy? 用 What does the\npassage say 直接问原文\"说了什么\",是细节复述题。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about machine accuracy? 是疑问代词 What 作宾语提前的\n特殊疑问句,主干\"the passage say about machine accuracy\",问\"文章关于机器准确率说了\n什么\"。无陷阱。\n【定位】As the databases grew, machine accuracy dipped across the board. Algorithms that were right\n95% of the time when they were dealing with a 13,000-image database, for example, were\naccurate about 70% of the time when confronted with 1 million images.\n随着数据库不断扩大,机器准确率全面下降。例如,当处理 13000 张图的数据库时准确率为\n95% 的算法,在面对 100 万张图时只有约 70% 的准确率。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt falls short of researchers' expectations.\"未达研究者预期\"与原文相反——Much\nbetter than we expected 明说\"比我们预期的好得多\",而非\"未达预期\"。\nB) 无中生有\nIt improves with added computing power.\"随计算能力增加而提升\"无原文支撑——\n原文只谈\"数据库规模 vs 准确率\",从未涉及 computing power(算力)这一变量。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt varies greatly with different algorithms.\"不同算法之间差异大\"以偏概全;原文\nAlgorithms that were right 95% of the time...were accurate about 70% 描述的\n是\"同一(批)算法在不同库规模下\"的差异,不是\"不同算法之间\"的差异。\nD) ✓ 正确\nAs the databases grew ↔ as the database size increases\nmachine accuracy dipped across the board ↔ It decreases\n预判的\"库越大,准确率越降\"与 It decreases as the database size increases. 完全等值,\n锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is said to be a shortcoming of facial-recognition machines?", "options": ["A) They cannot easily tell apart people with near-identical appearances.", "B) They have difficulty identifying changes in facial expressions.", "C) They are not sensitive to minute changes in people's mood.", "D) They have problems distinguishing people of the same age."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is said to be a shortcoming of facial-recognition machines? 问被明说的具\n体短板,需要逐字对应原文,而非提炼或推断。\n【拆句】What is said to be a shortcoming of facial-recognition machines? 是 What 作主语补\n足语的被动疑问句,主干 What is said to be a shortcoming,字面意思\"什么被说成是……的\n一个短板\"。be said to do 是固定句式,等于\"据说\"。\n【定位】Machines also had difficulty adjusting for people who look a lot alike—either\ndoppelgangers(长相极相似的人), whom the machine would have trouble identifying as two\nseparate people, or the same person who appeared in different photos at different ages or in\ndifferent lighting, whom the machine would incorrectly view as separate people.\n机器在处理长相非常相像的人时也有困难——要么是长相极相似的人,机器很难把他们识别\n为两个不同的人;要么是同一个人在不同年龄或不同光线下拍摄的照片,机器会错误地把他们\n视作不同的人。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\npeople who look a lot alike ↔ people with near-identical appearances\nhave trouble identifying as two separate people ↔ cannot easily tell apart\n预判的\"机器分辨不清长相相近的不同人\"与 They cannot easily tell apart people with\nnear-identical appearances. 在主语、动作、对象三层逐一对应,锁定 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey have difficulty identifying changes in facial expressions.\"难以识别面部表情的\n变化\"偷换概念;原文 P6 谈的是\"不同年龄/光线下的照片\",不是\"facial expressions(表\n情)\";\"年龄/光线\"≠\"表情\"。\nC) 无中生有\nThey are not sensitive to minute changes in people's mood.\"对人情绪的细微变化\n不敏感\"无原文支撑;原文 algorithms must be sensitive to tiny changes in identities\n谈的是\"身份的细微差异\",不是 mood(情绪)。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey have problems distinguishing people of the same age.\"难以区分同龄人\"偷换\n原文 different ages(不同年龄)的变量;原文是\"同一人在不同年龄/光线下被错认为不同\n人\",不是\"不同人在同一年龄下\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the difficulty confronting researchers of facial-recognition machines?", "options": ["A) No computer is yet able to handle huge datasets of human faces.", "B) There do not exist public databases with sufficient face samples.", "C) There are no appropriate algorithms to process the face samples.", "D) They have trouble converting face datasets into the right format."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the difficulty confronting researchers of facial-recognition machines?\n问的是研究者面临的具体困难,选项是其延续,属客观复述。\n【拆句】What is the difficulty confronting researchers of facial-recognition machines? 主干\nWhat is the difficulty,confronting researchers... 是现在分词短语作 difficulty 的后置定\n语,逻辑上 difficulty 是 confront 的主语——\"使研究者面临困境的那个困难是什么\"。\n【定位】The trouble is, for many of the researchers who'd like to design systems to address these\nchallenges, massive datasets for experimentation just don't exist—at least, not in formats that\nare accessible to academic researchers.\n麻烦在于,对许多想设计系统来应对这些挑战的研究者来说,用于实验的大规模数据集根本就\n不存在——至少不存在学术研究者能够获取的格式。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nNo computer is yet able to handle huge datasets of human faces.\"还没有电脑能处\n理巨型数据集\"偷换概念;原文 Give the right computer a massive database of faces,\nand it can process what it sees 反而说电脑\"能\"处理;研究者的困难是\"没有数据集\",不\n是\"电脑算不动\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmassive datasets for experimentation just don't exist ↔\nThere do not exist public databases with sufficient face samples.\nThere are no public databases that contain millions of faces. ↔\npublic databases with sufficient face samples\n预判的\"学术研究者拿不到大规模公开人脸数据集\"与 There do not exist public\ndatabases with sufficient face samples. 在\"不存在 + 公开 + 足量 + 数据集\"四点全部\n对应,锁定 B。\nC) 无中生有\nThere are no appropriate algorithms to process the face samples.\"没有合适的算\n法\"无原文支撑;原文 tested a variety of facial-recognition algorithms 说算法\"很多\n种\",且文中所有困难都指向\"数据缺失\"而非\"算法缺失\"。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey have trouble converting face datasets into the right format.\"难以把数据集转\n换成正确格式\"偷换概念;原文 not in formats that are accessible to academic\nresearchers 只是描述\"现有数据集不是研究者能拿到的格式\",并未说研究者要去\"转换格\n式\"(convert),且\"转换\"动作的主语应是数据所有者而非研究者。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author think of college students funding their education through loans?", "options": ["A) They only expect to get huge returns.", "B) They are acting in an irrational way.", "C) They benefit at taxpayers' expense.", "D) They will regret doing so someday."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of college students funding their education through\nloans? 问的是作者对一个具体行为的看法，定位明确、不要求跨段整合，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author think of college students，后面 funding their education\nthrough loans 是现在分词作 students 的后置定语，相当于 \"who are funding…\"，理解\n为 \"用借款交学费的大学生\"，分词结构不是另一句话，别把 funding 当主句动词读。\n【定位】Given that there's now over $1.3 trillion in student loans on the books, it's pretty clear that\nmany students are far from sensible.\n鉴于目前学生贷款账面上已经超过 1.3 万亿美元，相当明显，许多学生远谈不上理性。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey only expect to get huge returns. 把作者只谈到的 \"贷款行为不理智\" 偷换为 \"指\n望高回报\"，原文从头到尾没出现 returns / profit 之类表述。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfar from sensible ↔ acting in an irrational way\n预判要的是 \"不理智\"，irrational 正是 far from sensible 的字面同义，没有其他选项能\n在定位句里找到落点。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey benefit at taxpayers' expense. 把后面 P3 才出现的 \"纳税人买单\" 提前塞进 51\n题；但 P1 仅说学生 far from sensible，并未说他们正在占纳税人便宜。\nD) 过度推断\nThey will regret doing so someday. 把 \"不理智\" 推到 \"将来会后悔\"，多走了一步因\n果，P1 既没说 regret，也没说 someday。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "In the author's opinion, free college education is ________.", "options": ["A) Impractical", "B) Unsustainable", "C) A goal to strive for", "D) A way to social equality"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】In the author's opinion, free college education is ________. 是一个待补全的判断句，问\n作者对一个具体概念的判定，仍属细节题。\n【拆句】标准的 \"主语＋系动词＋表语空位\" 结构，空位填一个形容词或名词性短语充当表语；In the\nauthor's opinion 是插入语，去掉不影响主干。\n【定位】But the only way college could be free is if the faculty and staff donated their time, the\nbuildings required no maintenance, and campuses required no utilities. As long as it's\nimpossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier.\n但大学要 \"免费\" 的唯一可能是教职工无偿付出时间、楼宇不需维护、校园不耗水电。只要\n无中生有不可能，成本就绝对是一道门槛。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nit's impossible to produce something from nothing, costs are absolutely a barrier ↔\nimpractical\n预判要的是 \"做不到\"，而 impractical 字面就是 \"不切实际 / 行不通\"，与作者反事实条\n件＋ impossible 的判定一一对位。\nB) 偷换概念\nunsustainable 暗示 \"先能办、后维持不下去\"，原文是 impossible to produce\nsomething from nothing，从一开始就办不到，不是维持问题。\nC) 与原文相反\na goal to strive for 把作者否定的 \"免费\" 包装成值得奋斗的目标，与 costs are\nabsolutely a barrier 的彻底否定背道而驰。\nD) 无中生有\na way to social equality 把 free college 与 social equality 强行挂钩，但 P2 通篇只论\n可行性、不谈平等；此话题在文中始终缺席。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What should students do if taxpayers are to bear their college costs?", "options": ["A) Work even harder to repay society.", "B) Choose their subjects more carefully.", "C) Choose majors that will serve society's practical needs.", "D) Allow taxpayers to participate in college administration."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What should students do if taxpayers are to bear their college costs? 问的是一个条件\n下的具体行为建议，作者已在文中给出明确答复，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What should students do；if taxpayers are to bear their college costs 是 if 引导\n的条件状语从句，be to do 表 \"被安排 / 注定要\"，意为 \"假如让纳税人来承担学费\"。\n【定位】If we're going to force taxpayers to foot the bill for college degrees, students should only\nstudy those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers.\n假如我们要让纳税人为大学学位买单，学生就只应学那些对纳税人最有利的科目。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nWork even harder to repay society. 把 \"选什么专业\" 偷换为 \"更努力工作\"；P4 谈的\n是 \"study what\"，根本不是 \"how hard to work\"。\nB) 以偏概全\nChoose their subjects more carefully. 只说 \"更谨慎选择\"，没说选择标准是 \"对社会有\n用\"；和 only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers 的硬约束\n比，缺少 \"为纳税人 / 为社会\" 这一关键限定。\nC) ✓ 正确\nstudents should only study those subjects that're of greatest benefit to taxpayers ↔\nChoose majors that will serve society's practical needs\n预判核心是 \"挑对社会有用的专业\"。Choose majors 对应 study those subjects，\nserve society's practical needs 对应 of greatest benefit to taxpayers，无遗漏。\nD) 偷换概念\nAllow taxpayers to participate in college administration. 把纳税人的发言权由\ntaxpayers should be able to decide what students will study 偷换为 college\nadministration，越过了原文限定的范围。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say about the value of a student's college education?", "options": ["A) It is underestimated by profit-seeking employers.", "B) It is to be proved by what they can do on the job.", "C) It is well reflected in their average starting salary.", "D) It is embodied in how they remove social barriers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the value of a student's college education? 问作者\n就 value 给出的具体表述，定位单点、属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say about the value；of a student's college education 是\n介词短语作 value 的后置定语，整体翻成 \"学生大学教育的价值\"。\n【定位】And the value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing\nto pay the student to produce it.\n而学生能产出之物的价值，正反映在雇主愿意支付给学生让其去产出的那份工资里。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt is underestimated by profit-seeking employers. 把 the wage employers are\nwilling to pay 中性事实表述偷换为带贬义的 \"被逐利雇主低估\"，原文未出现\nunderestimate 或 profit-seeking 的负面定性。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt is to be proved by what they can do on the job. 把 reflected in the wage 偷换为\nproved by what they can do on the job，焦点从 \"工资多少\" 转成 \"工作表现\"，两者\n并不等同。\nC) ✓ 正确\nAverage starting salaries give a clear indication of what type of training society needs its new\nworkers to have\n↔ It is well reflected in their average starting salary\nthe value of what the student can produce is reflected in the wage employers are willing to pay\n↔ It is well reflected in their average starting salary\nP5 一上来谈 Average starting salaries，结尾再以 reflected in the wage 收束，\nreflected in … average starting salary 同时承接两处，是唯一全覆盖的选项。\nD) 无中生有\nIt is embodied in how they remove social barriers. 借用 Costs won't be a barrier\n中的 barrier 一词制造熟悉感，但 P5 既未谈 social barriers，也未谈 remove。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What message does the author want to convey in the passage?", "options": ["A) Students should think carefully whether to go to college.", "B) Taxpayers should only finance the most gifted students.", "C) The worth of a college education is open to debate.", "D) College students should fund their own education."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What message does the author want to convey in the passage? 既有 message 又有\nin the passage，两个主旨信号叠加，必走主线串联。\n【拆句】主干 What message does the author want to convey，in the passage 是地点状语限定\n范围；want to convey = 想要传达，无任何陷阱。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nStudents should think carefully whether to go to college. 把全文焦点 \"谁付钱\" 偷\n换为 \"是否上大学\"；P1 至 P9 从未质疑 \"上不上\" 这件事。\nB) 无中生有\nTaxpayers should only finance the most gifted students. 凭空加进 most gifted\nstudents 的资助标准；P4 谈的是 \"选有用的专业\"，不是 \"挑天才学生\"。\nC) 与原文相反\nThe worth of a college education is open to debate. 借用 The actual question we\ndebate 的 debate 一词制造熟悉感，但作者在 P9 里其实给出了**明确**结论 Either\nway, taxpayers have no business paying，态度毫不含糊，不是 \"open to debate\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nEither way, taxpayers have no business paying for students' college education. ↔\nCollege students should fund their own education.\nOr we can allow students both to choose their majors and pay for their education themselves ↔\nCollege students should fund their own education\nP8 的 Or 分支正面说 \"学生自选自付\"，P9 用 Either way 反向说 \"纳税人不该付\"，两句\n一正一反共同指向 College students should fund their own education，是唯一覆盖\n全篇主线的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
